00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:37.750
But, anyways, back to your question

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:41.340
about fish oil supplements
versus krill oil supplements.

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:44.100
So one of the major differences

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:46.160
is that fish oil supplements,

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:50.170
if you get a high quality one,
it's in a triglyceride form.

00:38:50.170 --> 00:38:53.400
So you've got a glycerol backbone

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:56.120
with three fatty acids that's attached,

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:58.293
and those are either DHA or the EPA.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.030
Or, if you have a lower
quality fish oil supplement,

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.810
then you have what's
called ethyl ester form.

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:08.490
And, typically, the reason for that,

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:10.750
when fish oil is purified,

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:13.780
it's run through this column
with alcohol or something.

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:15.660
They cleave it off, the glycerol backbone,

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.970
and then it's just kind of
easier to leave it like that

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:20.580
than re-esterifying it,
which costs more money.

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:22.580
So you can get it in ethyl ester form,

00:39:22.580 --> 00:39:25.550
which isn't as bioavailable.

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:28.160
And, in fact, if you
don't take it with food,

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:29.559
