There's DHA

00:51:56.483 --> 00:51:57.983
in the same supplement.
- Yes.

00:51:57.983 --> 00:51:58.970
- [Andrew] Is that okay?

00:51:58.970 --> 00:51:59.970
- Yes. Yeah, yeah.

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:02.070
And to kind of...

00:52:02.070 --> 00:52:04.050
Boy, we've got a lot of
things to hit back on

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:06.000
because one of your original questions

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:08.023
was krill oil versus fish oil.

00:52:08.023 --> 00:52:09.680
- Yes, still in the cue.

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:11.233
- DHA, specifically,

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:17.730
in phospholipid form,
it's more bioavailable.

00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:22.730
So, if you're comparing exact
quantity or concentration

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:27.070
in triglyceride form
versus phospholipid form,

00:52:27.070 --> 00:52:30.380
you will get more in your plasma cells,

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:31.343
in your plasma, plasma cell,

00:52:31.343 --> 00:52:34.240
in your plasma with krill oil.

00:52:34.240 --> 00:52:39.240
However, krill oil
supplements are so low dose.

00:52:39.620 --> 00:52:43.410
I mean, good luck getting two grams

00:52:43.410 --> 00:52:45.550
of omega-3 from krill oil.

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:49.120
And also, krill oil supplements
are notoriously rancid.

00:52:49.120 --> 00:52:50.160
I don't know for whatever reason.

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:52.557
- Maybe that's what
made me itchy all over.

00:52:52.557 --> 00:52:54.480
- I think they're just...

00:52:54.480 --> 00:52:57.880
I haven't found a good
krill oil supplement.

00:52:57.880 --> 00:52:58.723
I pretty much stay away from it.

00:52:58.723 --> 00:53:00.460
I mean, if you smell it too,

00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:03.623
I mean, it just smells rancid.

00:53:04.940 --> 00:53:05.773
So, but the thing is,

00:53:05.773 --> 00:53:08.793
and I also published a
paper on this back in 2019,

00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:13.560
yeah, something like that,

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:18.070
about DHA in phospholipid
form getting into the brain

00:53:19.643 --> 00:53:22.170
through a different mechanism
than DHA in triglyceride form.

00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:24.200
And so it's going through a transporter

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:26.090
called the MFSD2A transporter.

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:27.420
And I think it's very relevant for people

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:30.220
with an APOE4 allele, so-

00:53:30.220 --> 00:53:32.130
- People with an
Alzheimer's susceptibility.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:36.140
- Right, so like 25% of the
population has an allele

00:53:36.140 --> 00:53:37.543
and a gene called APOE4,

00:53:38.731 --> 00:53:43.730
and, basically, it's APOE,
but the four is referred to

00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:46.970
as the bad kind of version of it.

00:53:46.970 --> 00:53:49.800
This is something in our
bodies. It's also in our brain.

00:53:49.800 --> 00:53:52.190
And if people have one of these versions,

00:53:52.190 --> 00:53:53.880
if they got one from
their mom or their dad,

00:53:53.880 --> 00:53:56.220
they have a twofold increased
risk for Alzheimer's disease.

00:53:56.220 --> 00:53:59.577
If they get two, which is much more,

00:53:59.577 --> 00:54:00.790
it's less common.

00:54:00.790 --> 00:54:02.637
I think it's like 2% of
the population or something

00:54:02.637 --> 00:54:04.437
has two alleles, but they have like a 10

00:54:04.437 --> 00:54:06.780
or 11-fold increased risk
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:54:06.780 --> 00:54:11.470
So there is a role for
phospholipid form DHA in the brain,

00:54:11.470 --> 00:54:13.660
but you also make phospholipid
DHA inside your body.

00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:16.910
And you can do that by taking
in more triglyceride forms.

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:21.663
So two grams or more is
the magic number, I think.

00:54:24.185 --> 00:54:26.823
So kind of back to the why for fish oil.

00:54:27.850 --> 00:54:31.113
I personally think it is
one of the most powerful,

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:36.410
anti-inflammatory things,
dietary lifestyle,

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:40.960
things that we can get
easily, relatively easily,

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:45.430
that is going to powerfully
modulate the way you think,

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:48.330
the way you feel, and the way you age.

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:52.523
And a variety of
different types of studies

00:54:52.523 --> 00:54:54.650
kind of led me to that conclusion,

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:57.280
a variety of observational studies.

00:54:57.280 --> 00:54:59.380
So there's been lots of
work by Dr. Bill Harris

00:54:59.380 --> 00:55:01.860
and his collaborators looking at,

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:03.840
it's called the Omega-3 Index.

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:07.860
So this is actually the omega-3
level in red blood cells.

00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:10.900
So red blood cells turn
over about every 120 days.

00:55:10.900 --> 00:55:14.100
So it's a long-term
marker of omega-3 status.

00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:19.100
This is very different from
99.9% of any study you see

00:55:19.570 --> 00:55:23.320
or any lab that you go to to
get your omega-3 levels tested.

00:55:23.320 --> 00:55:25.920
You're getting your plasma
phospholipid levels tested,

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:28.470
which is kind of like
you can think of it as,

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:30.090
what did I eat a couple days before?

00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:33.240
Oh, I had fish. My
omega-3 levels are great.

00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:36.060
But did you eat fish like that every week,

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:38.710
or was it like you went out to dinner?

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:43.710
So it's not a great biomarker
for long-term omega-3 status.

00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:47.640
It's kind of like the
fasting blood glucose levels

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:50.543
versus the HbA1c, which
is like a long-term marker

00:55:50.543 --> 00:55:52.280
of your blood glucose levels.

00:55:52.280 --> 00:55:53.870
So the Omega-3 Index.

00:55:53.870 --> 00:55:56.780
He's done a variety of
studies, observational studies.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:57.640
So, for people listening,

00:55:57.640 --> 00:56:00.430
these are studies that
are obviously flawed

00:56:00.430 --> 00:56:04.330
because they're not
establishing causality.

00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:08.060
You're looking at people's lifestyles.

00:56:08.060 --> 00:56:10.640
But, in the case of Bill Harris's work,

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:11.830
he's measuring something.

00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:13.957
So he's measuring the Omega-3 Index.

00:56:13.957 --> 00:56:16.550
And he's measuring the
Omega-3 Index in people,

00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:18.860
and then looking at their
mortality risk, for example,

00:56:18.860 --> 00:56:20.550
or their cardiovascular disease risk.

00:56:20.550 --> 00:56:23.223
And what he has found is that,

00:56:24.150 --> 00:56:27.130
first of all, standard American diet

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:29.770
has an Omega-3 Index of 5%.

00:56:29.770 --> 00:56:32.860
Japan, by contrast, has an Omega-3 Index

00:56:32.860 --> 00:56:35.743
of around 10 to 11%.

00:56:35.743 --> 00:56:36.710
Big difference there.

00:56:36.710 --> 00:56:39.090
And they also have about a five-year

00:56:39.090 --> 00:56:42.600
increased life expectancy
compared to people in the US.

00:56:42.600 --> 00:56:44.600
- Do you think that's mainly
due to their fish intake,

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:45.433
seafood intake?

00:56:45.433 --> 00:56:47.040
- So what he showed was,

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:49.650
I think it's a big part of it.

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:51.650
I mean, you can't says
it's the only thing,

00:56:51.650 --> 00:56:54.060
but what he showed in his data was that,

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:56.870
and I think it was Framingham Study,

00:56:56.870 --> 00:56:59.027
where he looked at the Omega-3 Index,

00:56:59.027 --> 00:57:03.480
and people that had a
Omega-3 Index of 4% or lower,

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:04.313
so close to what the standard American is

00:57:04.313 --> 00:57:05.953
but a little bit lower,

00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:10.870
they had a five-year
decreased life expectancy

00:57:10.870 --> 00:57:14.560
compared to people that
had an 8% Omega-3 Index.

00:57:14.560 --> 00:57:17.138
And so big difference there, right?

00:57:17.138 --> 00:57:18.960
Five years life expectancy.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:21.010
But here's the really
interesting thing, Andrew.

00:57:21.010 --> 00:57:22.493
He also looked at smokers,

00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:24.727
