fish a couple times a week.

00:59:23.810 --> 00:59:26.360
I do want to just touch on
food sources for a moment.

00:59:27.490 --> 00:59:31.750
First of all, are there plants
that are rich in omega-3s?

00:59:31.750 --> 00:59:36.233
And second, I have some friends
who are really into meat.

00:59:37.420 --> 00:59:39.410
I like meat a lot, my dad's Argentine,

00:59:39.410 --> 00:59:40.860
but I don't eat very much of it.

00:59:40.860 --> 00:59:42.070
I try and eat high quality meats

00:59:42.070 --> 00:59:46.480
in relatively limited amounts,
but I do eat pretty often.

00:59:46.480 --> 00:59:50.830
But I've been told by these sources

00:59:50.830 --> 00:59:53.000
of a questionable authority that,

00:59:53.000 --> 00:59:56.550
if an animal grazes on really
good grasses, for instance,

00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:59.300
that the meat can contain
a lot of omega-3s,

00:59:59.300 --> 01:00:01.100
which, in principle, makes sense

01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:02.520
based on this Omega-3 Index.

01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:04.440
'Cause you're telling me
that a lot of this omega-3

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:06.600
is sequestered into the red blood cells.

01:00:06.600 --> 01:00:09.220
So, if I'm eating high
quality, grass-fed meat

01:00:09.220 --> 01:00:11.850
and the grasses had omega-3s,

01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:14.840
do my steaks have omega-3s or no?

01:00:14.840 --> 01:00:16.750
- So there was a study published

01:00:16.750 --> 01:00:19.710
that compared conventional meat,

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:24.710
so meat that animals are fed
corn or soy or whatever it is.

01:00:25.220 --> 01:00:26.840
- Which is terrible.
- [Rhonda] Yeah.

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:29.270
- For animals and people,
as far as I can tell.

01:00:29.270 --> 01:00:32.480
I'm sure I'll get some
attacks, but that's okay.

01:00:32.480 --> 01:00:33.880
I won't read those comments.

01:00:35.400 --> 01:00:37.160
Again, a joke. I read all the comments.

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:41.050
But it seems to me that these animals

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:43.730
have to either be taking fish oil

01:00:43.730 --> 01:00:47.070
or eat plants that are
very rich in omega-3s

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:48.400
in order for the meat

01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:50.684
to actually contain sufficient omega-3s.

01:00:50.684 --> 01:00:53.010
- So the meat, comparing
the conventional meat

01:00:53.010 --> 01:00:58.010
to the grass-fed or
pasture-raised cows or cattle,

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:03.140
there were higher levels
of alpha-linolenic acid.

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:08.140
And ALA, it can be
converted into EPA and DHA,

01:01:09.430 --> 01:01:13.220
but the conversion is very inefficient

01:01:13.220 --> 01:01:15.930
and very dependent on
a variety of factors,

01:01:15.930 --> 01:01:17.660
including genetics.

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:20.830
Genetics, a huge regulator.

01:01:20.830 --> 01:01:23.210
Some people can do it much better.

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:27.990
Others, you're getting like
5% of conversion to EPA.

01:01:27.990 --> 01:01:32.990
Estrogen is a major regulator
of making that more efficient.

01:01:33.020 --> 01:01:35.220
It makes sense because pregnancy,

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:37.533
when your estrogen just
goes through the roof.

01:01:38.660 --> 01:01:40.740
These omega-3 fatty acids
play a very important role

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:41.790
in brain development.

01:01:43.686 --> 01:01:47.250
Women are supposed to be
converting any ALA they can

01:01:47.250 --> 01:01:50.261
into the longer-chain omega-3 fatty acids.

01:01:50.261 --> 01:01:53.340
So estrogen does affect that,

01:01:53.340 --> 01:01:55.130
but I would say plant sources.

01:01:55.130 --> 01:01:57.910
So, if you're looking for the ALA,

01:01:57.910 --> 01:02:01.380
plant sources would be walnuts, flaxseeds.

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:03.060
Those are probably the highest.

01:02:03.060 --> 01:02:06.830
But, if a person is a
vegan or a vegetarian,

01:02:06.830 --> 01:02:10.140
their best bet is to
actually get microalgae oil.

01:02:10.140 --> 01:02:12.070
And you can supplement with microalgae oil

01:02:12.070 --> 01:02:16.740
because microalgae, they do make the DHA.

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:20.650
And so that would be a better source

01:02:20.650 --> 01:02:23.900
for people that are vegetarian and vegan

01:02:23.900 --> 01:02:26.320
rather than doing the flaxseed oil

01:02:26.320 --> 01:02:28.430
because that conversion inefficiency,

01:02:28.430 --> 01:02:32.300
the enzymes that convert
ALA into EPA and DHA,

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:33.720
again, it's inefficient.

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:35.653
- And then, for people that eat fish,

01:02:36.490 --> 01:02:38.420
sardines, you said.

01:02:38.420 --> 01:02:39.253
- [Rhonda] Salmon.

01:02:39.253 --> 01:02:41.610
- Salmon, and you have to eat
the skin, as I understand.

01:02:42.473 --> 01:02:44.248
- You don't have to, but it's good.

01:02:44.248 --> 01:02:45.190
- [Andrew] It's rich with omegas.

01:02:45.190 --> 01:02:46.563
- The oil, yeah.

01:02:46.563 --> 01:02:48.430
And the reason I say,

01:02:48.430 --> 01:02:52.210
I think the best would
be wild Alaskan salmon

01:02:52.210 --> 01:02:54.440
versus the farm-raised because
the farm-raised, again,

01:02:54.440 --> 01:02:57.270
they're feeding 'em corn.

01:02:57.270 --> 01:02:58.857
They're feeding 'em grain and stuff.

01:02:58.857 --> 01:02:59.690
- [Andrew] Really?

01:02:59.690 --> 01:03:01.227
- And then, they give 'em astaxanthin.

01:03:01.227 --> 01:03:02.880
So astaxanthin is a carotenoid.

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:05.030
It's the carotenoid that's
in things like krill,

01:03:05.030 --> 01:03:07.200
crustaceans that make their red pigment.

01:03:07.200 --> 01:03:10.047
- Yeah, it's also being
used now as a supplement,

01:03:10.047 --> 01:03:12.040
and there's a prescription form

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:15.240
to try and rescue some
age-related vision loss

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:18.540
because of the role of
the vitamin A pathway

01:03:18.540 --> 01:03:20.150
and photoreceptors.

01:03:20.150 --> 01:03:22.580
- Yeah, well, actually the
carotinoids themselves,

01:03:22.580 --> 01:03:23.920
so like lutein, zeaxathin,

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:26.760
they're really good at
sequestering singlet oxygen,

01:03:26.760 --> 01:03:28.760
which is damaging, right?

01:03:28.760 --> 01:03:30.740
- Yeah, as we age,

01:03:30.740 --> 01:03:33.730
because the retinal cells,
the cells of the eye

01:03:33.730 --> 01:03:35.260
are so metabolically active,

01:03:35.260 --> 01:03:38.240
they accumulate a lot of
reactive oxygen species,

01:03:38.240 --> 01:03:40.860
and mitochondrial repair

01:03:40.860 --> 01:03:43.410
and limiting reactive oxygen species

01:03:43.410 --> 01:03:46.963
is a major theme of
trying to rescue vision.

01:03:46.963 --> 01:03:49.160
That's a whole other podcast and story.

01:03:49.160 --> 01:03:50.600
There's some really interesting data now

01:03:50.600 --> 01:03:52.340
on the use of red light to try and trigger

01:03:52.340 --> 01:03:54.300
these pathways.
- I've seen some.

01:03:54.300 --> 01:03:57.650
That's my good friend of many
years and amazing scientist

01:03:57.650 --> 01:04:00.650
Glen Jeffery's lab at
University College London.

01:04:00.650 --> 01:04:02.560
We should talk about that
at some point, if not today.

01:04:02.560 --> 01:04:04.357
- I saw that study, like 2020, was it?

01:04:04.357 --> 01:04:05.220
- Now, they have a second study.

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:06.180
- Oh, do they? They've done, okay.

01:04:06.180 --> 01:04:07.822
- It's looking real.

01:04:07.822 --> 01:04:09.460
- [Rhonda] That's exciting.
- I mean, they're cautious.

01:04:09.460 --> 01:04:13.020
They're appropriately British
and cautious about it.

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:15.130
I always joke if those studies
had been done over here,

01:04:15.130 --> 01:04:16.870
