01:06:53.790 --> 01:06:54.837
Okay, well, I want to get to that 8%.

01:06:54.837 --> 01:06:57.640
By the way, I'm almost 16% Omega-3 Index.

01:06:57.640 --> 01:07:00.121
- Yeah, I was going to ask about testing.

01:07:00.121 --> 01:07:05.121
So, where and how can somebody
measure their Omega-3 Index?

01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:07.150
Which, again, just to remind people,

01:07:07.150 --> 01:07:09.690
is essentially the percentage of omega-3s

01:07:09.690 --> 01:07:10.860
that you have in your blood

01:07:10.860 --> 01:07:15.400
with the caveat that the Omega-3 Index

01:07:15.400 --> 01:07:18.760
will be heavily biased by what
you ate in the previous days.

01:07:18.760 --> 01:07:19.980
- Not the Omega-3 Index.

01:07:19.980 --> 01:07:21.510
Okay, so, the Omega-3-
- Sorry, I misunderstood.

01:07:21.510 --> 01:07:22.830
I thought you said, in red blood cells,

01:07:22.830 --> 01:07:24.180
if I ate salmon two days ago,

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:25.860
my Omega-3 Index is going to go up.

01:07:25.860 --> 01:07:27.110
- No, that was plasma.

01:07:27.110 --> 01:07:28.248
- [Andrew] I misunderstood.

01:07:28.248 --> 01:07:30.550
- So most people are measuring,

01:07:30.550 --> 01:07:32.639
if you look at a lot of
studies, and honestly, Andrew,

01:07:32.639 --> 01:07:35.380
I think a lot of the
reason for conflicting data

01:07:35.380 --> 01:07:39.516
is because people are measuring
plasma omega-3 levels.

01:07:39.516 --> 01:07:40.349
- [Andrew] Okay.

01:07:40.349 --> 01:07:42.090
- The phospholipids. It's
in a phospholipid, right?

01:07:42.090 --> 01:07:44.300
Your phospholipids are carrying things.

01:07:44.300 --> 01:07:45.850
These are lipoproteins.

01:07:45.850 --> 01:07:47.520
They're carrying things like omega-3

01:07:47.520 --> 01:07:50.720
and triglycerides and stuff
and shuttling 'em around.

01:07:50.720 --> 01:07:53.720
The Omega-3 Index is actually
in the red blood cells,

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:56.830
and red blood cells take
120 days to turn over.

01:07:56.830 --> 01:07:59.710
So, if you're going to do a baseline test,

01:07:59.710 --> 01:08:02.680
if you want to know before
supplementing what your level is,

01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:06.110
you have to wait 120 days
before doing the second test

01:08:06.110 --> 01:08:08.190
after supplementing to
know how much you went up

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:10.570
because that's how long it takes

01:08:10.570 --> 01:08:12.390
for your red blood cell to turn over.

01:08:12.390 --> 01:08:14.460
So the Omega-3 Index.

01:08:14.460 --> 01:08:19.390
Bill Harris has a company
that he co-founded.

01:08:19.390 --> 01:08:21.350
It's called OmegaQuant,

01:08:21.350 --> 01:08:23.390
and they measure the Omega-3 Index.

01:08:23.390 --> 01:08:25.780
They have a variety of
different index tests.

01:08:25.780 --> 01:08:27.700
You can do a basic one or
a little more advanced.

01:08:27.700 --> 01:08:29.200
- This is from a blood draw.

01:08:29.200 --> 01:08:31.160
- It's a little blood spot thing, yeah.

01:08:31.160 --> 01:08:35.150
And he uses money to funnel
back into doing lipid research.

01:08:35.150 --> 01:08:37.700
He's out there doing all
sorts of interesting studies

01:08:37.700 --> 01:08:39.285
on omega-3s, so it's great.

01:08:39.285 --> 01:08:41.260
But the Omega-3 Index is great.

01:08:41.260 --> 01:08:44.050
I think that, honestly, more people

01:08:44.050 --> 01:08:46.210
and more researchers should be using it

01:08:46.210 --> 01:08:49.310
because the conflicting data,

01:08:49.310 --> 01:08:52.450
it always comes down to
what we're measuring,

01:08:52.450 --> 01:08:53.763
the sensitivity of it.

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:56.110
Are we even measuring anything?

01:08:56.110 --> 01:09:00.000
So you're giving someone
500 milligrams of DHA,

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:00.960
and you don't see any effect.

01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:02.800
Well, did you measure
what their levels were?

01:09:02.800 --> 01:09:05.840
And did you measure the Omega-3 Index?

01:09:05.840 --> 01:09:07.140
There's all sorts of problems

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:09.400
with randomized controlled trials,

01:09:09.400 --> 01:09:13.180
and I think that we
need to, as scientists,

01:09:13.180 --> 01:09:16.060
we need to come together
and make some progress.

01:09:16.060 --> 01:09:18.472
I mean, you know? Let's
all talk to each other.

01:09:18.472 --> 01:09:20.970
Let's figure things out.

01:09:20.970 --> 01:09:23.360
This test is out there. It should be used.

01:09:23.360 --> 01:09:26.260
It should be used not just by
Bill's group, but everyone.

01:09:26.260 --> 01:09:28.987
- Yeah, well, and I'm
learning so much from you.

01:09:28.987 --> 01:09:32.020
And I agree we need more collaboration.

01:09:32.020 --> 01:09:34.510
I've always enjoyed really
fruitful collaborations

01:09:34.510 --> 01:09:36.330
in my lab at Stanford.

01:09:36.330 --> 01:09:37.970
Collaborating is just so much more fun.

01:09:37.970 --> 01:09:41.640
Online, there seems to be a
bias more towards creating silos

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:43.800
as opposed to bridges,

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:46.910
but I appreciate that
you bring up the need

01:09:46.910 --> 01:09:47.830
for more collaboration.

01:09:47.830 --> 01:09:50.050
And knowing which measures are best,

01:09:50.050 --> 01:09:52.390
and, in this case, now, thank
you for the clarification,

01:09:52.390 --> 01:09:55.792
I understand this Omega-3
Index is going to be best.

01:09:55.792 --> 01:09:56.625
So, basically, now, when I look at you,

01:09:56.625 --> 01:09:59.130
I think, you are 16% omega-3.

01:09:59.130 --> 01:10:01.400
- And dolphins are 19%. I'm almost-

01:10:01.400 --> 01:10:03.153
- Is that your goal?
You're trying to get there.

01:10:03.153 --> 01:10:05.580
- It is. [laughs]

01:10:05.580 --> 01:10:06.750
- Interesting.

01:10:06.750 --> 01:10:08.159
Actually, they should
probably do something

01:10:08.159 --> 01:10:10.950
where you're trying to
achieve the omega-3 ratio

01:10:10.950 --> 01:10:13.083
of your favorite species.

01:10:14.290 --> 01:10:17.060
Now that we've covered a bit
of how to get these things

01:10:17.060 --> 01:10:20.070
into one system, depending
on what one eats, et cetera,

01:10:20.070 --> 01:10:21.870
and some of the better measurements,

01:10:22.920 --> 01:10:27.530
how is omega-3 and some of
these other related lipids,

01:10:27.530 --> 01:10:29.310
how are they having
these positive effects?

01:10:29.310 --> 01:10:33.120
In my mind, and this is
incredibly elementary,

01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:35.060
but my understanding
is that, at some level,

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:37.060
they're making platelets more slippery.

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:38.520
Is that true or not?

01:10:38.520 --> 01:10:40.460
I'm happy to be wrong.

01:10:40.460 --> 01:10:43.030
How is it possibly impacting my mood?

01:10:43.030 --> 01:10:45.870
Is it through the synthesis
of membrane on neurons

01:10:45.870 --> 01:10:48.530
that allows neurons to
release more transmitter,

01:10:48.530 --> 01:10:50.510
like serotonin and dopamine?

01:10:50.510 --> 01:10:53.680
What are some of the purported, reported,

01:10:53.680 --> 01:10:55.520
and known mechanisms?

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:59.640
- I think some of the
most well-known mechanisms

01:10:59.640 --> 01:11:02.760
do have to do with the omega-3 fatty acids

01:11:02.760 --> 01:11:07.540
being very powerful regulators
of the inflammatory process

01:11:07.540 --> 01:11:09.170
in some way, shape, or form,

01:11:09.170 --> 01:11:12.430
whether that has to do with
resolvins that are produced

01:11:12.430 --> 01:11:16.060
from the metabolites of DHA, for example.

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:19.840
Resolvins play a role in
resolving inflammation.

01:11:19.840 --> 01:11:22.360
You want your inflammatory
response to be activated

01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:23.580
when it's supposed to be,

01:11:23.580 --> 01:11:25.190
but you want to resolve that inflammation

01:11:25.190 --> 01:11:28.887
and the inflammatory response
in a timely manner, right?

01:11:28.887 --> 01:11:30.745
And resolvins help do that.

01:11:30.745 --> 01:11:32.680
And so resolvins are one,

01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:36.010
and then there's these specialized
pro-mediating molecules,

01:11:36.010 --> 01:11:39.460
the SPMs, that also help
resolve the inflammation.

01:11:39.460 --> 01:11:40.470
There's, like you mentioned,

01:11:40.470 --> 01:11:42.367
the leukotrienes and prostaglandins,

01:11:42.367 --> 01:11:44.070
and these things are
being affected by EPA,

01:11:44.070 --> 01:11:47.330
and they do affect platelets
and platelet aggregation,

01:11:47.330 --> 01:11:50.653
and they do affect that
whole pathway as well.

01:11:52.870 --> 01:11:57.180
I think there's just so many
different ways and inputs.

01:11:57.180 --> 01:11:58.970
And so, when we talk about inflammation,

01:11:58.970 --> 01:12:01.610
honestly, that's a big general term,

01:12:01.610 --> 01:12:04.630
but when you're talking
about serotonin release

01:12:05.513 --> 01:12:07.510
at the level of neurons,

01:12:07.510 --> 01:12:09.250
we know that these inflammatory molecules

01:12:09.250 --> 01:12:10.450
cross the blood-brain barrier.

01:12:10.450 --> 01:12:12.240
I just mentioned ago
about injecting people

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:15.123
with lipopolysaccharide and
causing and depressive symptoms.

01:12:16.260 --> 01:12:21.260
It's known that omega-3,
actually specifically EPA,

01:12:21.550 --> 01:12:23.930
is able to help serotonin.

01:12:23.930 --> 01:12:26.670
Inflammation inhibits
the release of serotonin,

01:12:26.670 --> 01:12:31.400
and so EPA is actually able to
blunt inflammatory responses

01:12:31.400 --> 01:12:32.490
along with DHA as well.

01:12:32.490 --> 01:12:34.410
DHA does that through resolvins and stuff.

01:12:34.410 --> 01:12:37.480
And this then helps more
serotonin be released

01:12:37.480 --> 01:12:40.150
because you're not having
so much inflammation

01:12:40.150 --> 01:12:41.300
getting into the brain

01:12:41.300 --> 01:12:43.170
and affecting serotonin release, right?

01:12:43.170 --> 01:12:44.060
That's one mechanism.

01:12:44.060 --> 01:12:44.980
And then, another would be,

01:12:44.980 --> 01:12:46.830
well, DHA itself has been shown,

01:12:46.830 --> 01:12:49.630
it's a very important fatty acid

01:12:49.630 --> 01:12:51.610
that makes up cell membranes,
many cell membranes,

01:12:51.610 --> 01:12:53.070
including in our neurons.

01:12:53.070 --> 01:12:55.100
And, as you very well know, Andrew,

01:12:55.100 --> 01:12:58.690
the structure and function
of receptors of transporters,

01:12:58.690 --> 01:13:02.640
these membrane-bound proteins
on the surface of our cells,

01:13:02.640 --> 01:13:03.900
including neurons,

01:13:03.900 --> 01:13:06.570
are affected by the membrane fluidity,

01:13:06.570 --> 01:13:10.840
like how rigid and how
fluid the cell membrane is,

01:13:10.840 --> 01:13:12.743
and DHA plays a role in that.

01:13:12.743 --> 01:13:14.650
And so, for example, in animal studies,

01:13:14.650 --> 01:13:17.250
if you make an animal deficient in DHA,

01:13:17.250 --> 01:13:19.990
their serotonin receptors,
dopamine receptors,

01:13:19.990 --> 01:13:23.570
they're affected because the
structure of them is affected

01:13:23.570 --> 01:13:25.940
through the fluidity of the membrane.

01:13:25.940 --> 01:13:29.290
And so I think that's another mechanism.

01:13:29.290 --> 01:13:31.290
And I'm talking sort of general

01:13:31.290 --> 01:13:32.640
'cause I'm not a neuroscientist.

01:13:32.640 --> 01:13:34.270
- No, but it makes perfect sense.

01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:39.270
We know, for instance,
neuroplasticity almost always

01:13:39.600 --> 01:13:41.440
involves the recruitment of more receptors

01:13:41.440 --> 01:13:43.930
or an improvement in some feature

01:13:43.930 --> 01:13:46.630
of receptors to neurotransmitters.

01:13:46.630 --> 01:13:49.290
And they literally move
laterally in the membrane.

01:13:49.290 --> 01:13:50.950
They kind of float
around like little rafts.

01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:53.476
Sometimes they are, in
fact, in lipid rafts.

01:13:53.476 --> 01:13:56.310
So it makes perfect sense
that these molecules like DHA,

01:13:56.310 --> 01:13:58.940
which are part of the
structural fat of the neuron,

01:13:58.940 --> 01:14:00.480
because, of course,
the outsides of neurons

01:14:00.480 --> 01:14:01.560
are basically fat,

01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:03.510
not just the myelin that
people have heard of,

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:07.970
but the actual membranes,
that getting that right,

01:14:07.970 --> 01:14:09.720
you wouldn't want it as rigid as concrete,

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:12.640
but you wouldn't want it as soft as...

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:13.807
Need to come up with something here.

01:14:13.807 --> 01:14:15.950
What's that gooey stuff
that kids play with?

01:14:15.950 --> 01:14:17.820
It's like that goo. Anyway.

01:14:17.820 --> 01:14:19.830
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah, it's disgusting,

01:14:19.830 --> 01:14:22.170
and it's too soft to be
a membrane for a neuron.

01:14:22.170 --> 01:14:23.960
- You get it in those machines,
like the claw machines.

01:14:23.960 --> 01:14:24.910
- Someone put it in the comments

01:14:24.910 --> 01:14:27.410
and tell me what that
disgusting gooey stuff is.

01:14:27.410 --> 01:14:29.660
You don't want your
neurons to be that gooey,

01:14:29.660 --> 01:14:32.082
and yet you don't want them
to be like concrete either.

01:14:32.082 --> 01:14:34.260
- It's a balance. Yeah.
- It's a balance.

01:14:34.260 --> 01:14:38.840
And, in mentioning DHA, I
realize I'm backtracking,

01:14:38.840 --> 01:14:40.100
but I want to make sure that we close

01:14:40.100 --> 01:14:42.070
all the hatches for people.

01:14:42.070 --> 01:14:44.530
We talked a lot about EPA,

01:14:44.530 --> 01:14:47.210
but are food sources of DHA

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:51.090
that you find particularly attractive,

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:53.851
either by taste or by potency for DHA,

01:14:53.851 --> 01:14:55.930
what are just a few
that we could throw out?

01:14:55.930 --> 01:14:58.590
Because I am aware that
there are supplements

01:14:58.590 --> 01:15:00.990
where you can get a nice
ratio of EPA to DHA,

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:02.740
or you take them separately, as you do.

01:15:02.740 --> 01:15:04.823
But if I want to make sure
that I'm getting enough DHA,

01:15:04.823 --> 01:15:07.290
what do I need to be sure I'm
eating on a regular basis?

01:15:07.290 --> 01:15:11.284
- Well, the fish is packaging
the DHA and EPA in the ratio,

01:15:11.284 --> 01:15:16.284
but I also do eat salmon
roe, which is very salty,

01:15:16.370 --> 01:15:18.750
and it's a really high source

01:15:18.750 --> 01:15:22.260
of the phosphatidylcholine DHA

01:15:22.260 --> 01:15:23.490
that we talked about.
- So this is fish eggs.

01:15:23.490 --> 01:15:24.970
- It is, and actually-

01:15:24.970 --> 01:15:26.585
- [Andrew] That I like for some reason.

01:15:26.585 --> 01:15:27.418
- Oh, do you?

01:15:27.418 --> 01:15:30.430
- Yeah, so I'm discovering
something about myself.

01:15:30.430 --> 01:15:33.270
This was not meant to be
nutritional psychotherapy,

01:15:33.270 --> 01:15:34.930
but you're doing that for me anyway.

01:15:34.930 --> 01:15:37.620
I'm discovering that, yeah,
I like eating embryonic fish.

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:39.530
I just don't like eating the actual fish.

01:15:39.530 --> 01:15:40.600
- Okay, well.

01:15:40.600 --> 01:15:43.720
- Okay, so fish eggs are
okay. So caviar, basically.

01:15:43.720 --> 01:15:44.570
- Caviar, yes.

01:15:44.570 --> 01:15:47.000
And that's a good source
of the phospholipid form.

01:15:47.000 --> 01:15:48.480
And I was consuming that a lot

01:15:48.480 --> 01:15:50.513
because I wanted to get
the phospholipid form.

01:15:50.513 --> 01:15:51.510
- [Andrew] Yum!

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:52.530
- And it's actually really good.

01:15:52.530 --> 01:15:53.820
There's been some animal studies

01:15:53.820 --> 01:15:55.880
in piglets and rodents as well

01:15:55.880 --> 01:15:59.560
showing that consuming phospholipid DHA

01:16:01.250 --> 01:16:03.290
during fetal brain development

01:16:03.290 --> 01:16:06.130
gets like 10 times more DHA in the brain.

01:16:06.130 --> 01:16:07.210
Again, it's-

01:16:07.210 --> 01:16:09.890
- Makes sense based on fetal development.

01:16:09.890 --> 01:16:13.470
So, do I need to buy beluga caviar?

01:16:13.470 --> 01:16:14.650
Stuff can get pretty expensive

01:16:14.650 --> 01:16:17.070
at $200 a tin.
- I don't think you need to.

01:16:17.070 --> 01:16:20.160
I think it's a matter of preference.

01:16:20.160 --> 01:16:22.370
And if you're supplementing

01:16:22.370 --> 01:16:25.290
with your two to four grams of fish oil,

01:16:25.290 --> 01:16:28.480
I mean, you're going to get
phospholipid form anyway

01:16:28.480 --> 01:16:29.630
'cause your body's going to make it.

01:16:29.630 --> 01:16:31.530
- Okay, I've seen some containers

01:16:31.530 --> 01:16:34.170
of what I assume to be quality fish eggs

01:16:34.170 --> 01:16:36.650
that are not at the caviar level

01:16:36.650 --> 01:16:38.710
that you can find in the
better grocery stores

01:16:38.710 --> 01:16:40.095
that aren't super expensive.

01:16:40.095 --> 01:16:40.928
- [Rhonda] Right.

01:16:40.928 --> 01:16:42.120
- I wouldn't dip as low as to go eat,

01:16:42.120 --> 01:16:44.400
for instance, like fishing bait.

01:16:44.400 --> 01:16:45.850
Like, when we were kids,
we used to go fishing,

01:16:45.850 --> 01:16:47.140
and you'd put the fish egg on the thing.

01:16:47.140 --> 01:16:48.340
That's probably not good.
- No.

01:16:48.340 --> 01:16:51.280
- Although it's good enough
for the fish apparently. Okay.

01:16:51.280 --> 01:16:52.570
Only half joking here, folks.

01:16:52.570 --> 01:16:54.530
I'm just trying to protect
you from yourselves.

01:16:54.530 --> 01:16:57.130
Don't get any crazy ideas
about eating fishing bait.

01:16:58.064 --> 01:17:00.620
Okay, so that's great to know.

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:02.370
So we have these plant-based compounds,

01:17:02.370 --> 01:17:04.840
we have the omega-3s, so EPA, DHA,

01:17:04.840 --> 01:17:07.220
and then you mentioned
there's a third category.

01:17:07.220 --> 01:17:09.240
What would you place
in your third category

01:17:09.240 --> 01:17:14.230
of foods or supplement-based nutrients

01:17:14.230 --> 01:17:17.820
that our health, brain and/or body health,

01:17:17.820 --> 01:17:19.200
can really benefit from?

01:17:19.200 --> 01:17:21.600
- I mean, I think the most
obvious would be vitamin D,

01:17:21.600 --> 01:17:24.793
which is actually, as you
know, a steroid hormone

01:17:24.793 --> 01:17:28.040
that we produce when we're in the sun.

01:17:28.040 --> 01:17:31.750
Depending on the time of year,
we can make it in our skin.

01:17:31.750 --> 01:17:34.220
And depending on how much
melanin we have on our skin,

01:17:34.220 --> 01:17:37.103
or whether or not we're wearing
sunscreen or how old we are,

01:17:38.230 --> 01:17:41.290
there's a sliding scale on
how efficient that process is.

01:17:41.290 --> 01:17:43.600
- As I understand, there's
an inverse relationship,

01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:48.290
where the darker your skin is naturally,

01:17:48.290 --> 01:17:50.470
the more vitamin D you need to consume.

01:17:50.470 --> 01:17:51.370
Is that right?

01:17:51.370 --> 01:17:54.490
- Well, the darker your
skin is, the harder it is.

01:17:54.490 --> 01:17:56.890
So there was a study out of
the University of Chicago,

01:17:56.890 --> 01:17:57.880
this was several years ago,

01:17:57.880 --> 01:18:00.690
where they looked at African Americans

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:04.000
and compared African
Americans to Caucasians

01:18:04.000 --> 01:18:05.550
with light skin, fair skin,

01:18:05.550 --> 01:18:09.010
and how well they could make
vitamin D from sun exposure,

01:18:09.010 --> 01:18:13.350
and how long they had to be
in the sun to make X amount.

01:18:13.350 --> 01:18:15.620
And it turns out that African Americans

01:18:15.620 --> 01:18:17.150
with darker pigmentation,

01:18:17.150 --> 01:18:19.030
which protects them from
the burning rays of the sun,

01:18:19.030 --> 01:18:20.470
it's a natural sunscreen,

01:18:20.470 --> 01:18:23.840
had to stay in the sun six times as long

01:18:23.840 --> 01:18:26.930
as someone with none of
that natural sunscreen.

01:18:26.930 --> 01:18:31.930
So I think the take-home
there is a lot of people

01:18:32.160 --> 01:18:34.640
with darker skin living
in Sub-Saharan Africa,

01:18:34.640 --> 01:18:37.160
or people living in
India with darker skin,

01:18:37.160 --> 01:18:38.220
or in the Philippines,

01:18:38.220 --> 01:18:40.154
you know, these equatorial regions

01:18:40.154 --> 01:18:42.630
where you tend to see darker skin

01:18:42.630 --> 01:18:44.340
because it's protection from
the burning rays of the sun.

01:18:44.340 --> 01:18:45.200
- An adaptation.

01:18:45.200 --> 01:18:47.194
- They are in the sun more,

01:18:47.194 --> 01:18:48.850
and they're getting more vitamin D,

01:18:48.850 --> 01:18:51.220
but people that maybe
moved to United States,

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:55.610
to like Minnesota, or in a
place where UVB radiation

01:18:55.610 --> 01:18:58.890
isn't getting to the atmosphere
12 months out of the year.

01:18:58.890 --> 01:19:01.980
It's only getting there
four months, for example.

01:19:01.980 --> 01:19:03.470
Or even living in our modern day society,

01:19:03.470 --> 01:19:04.960
where people just don't
go outside anymore.

01:19:04.960 --> 01:19:05.793
I mean, we're inside.

01:19:05.793 --> 01:19:07.760
We're at our laptops in school.

01:19:07.760 --> 01:19:09.500
We're at work. We're in
our cubicle, whatever.

01:19:09.500 --> 01:19:12.370
So supplementation does play a major role,

01:19:12.370 --> 01:19:15.030
not only for people with darker skin

01:19:15.030 --> 01:19:16.980
that aren't outside all
the time, but for everyone.

01:19:16.980 --> 01:19:21.840
70% of the US population has
inadequate vitamin D levels.

01:19:21.840 --> 01:19:24.340
70 of the whole US.
- [Andrew] Amazing.

01:19:24.340 --> 01:19:25.810
- So this is everyone.

01:19:25.810 --> 01:19:28.760
And so I think that insufficient levels

01:19:28.760 --> 01:19:32.763
defined as less than 30
nanograms per milliliter,

01:19:33.902 --> 01:19:38.279
and that's defined by
the Endocrine Society,

01:19:38.279 --> 01:19:41.600
looking at a lot of
different aggregate studies,

01:19:41.600 --> 01:19:43.930
all-cause mortality, for example.

01:19:43.930 --> 01:19:46.120
There's been a lot of
different meta analyses

01:19:46.120 --> 01:19:47.610
of all-cause mortality studies,

01:19:47.610 --> 01:19:51.330
where vitamin D levels
really seem to be ideal

01:19:51.330 --> 01:19:54.820
between 40 to 60 nanograms per milliliter.

01:19:54.820 --> 01:19:57.940
And so, in order to get to that level,

01:19:57.940 --> 01:20:01.013
if you are not outside all the time,

01:20:01.013 --> 01:20:02.680
live in Southern California,

01:20:02.680 --> 01:20:04.870
where you're always outside
without sunscreen on.

01:20:04.870 --> 01:20:05.760
I always wear sunscreen

01:20:05.760 --> 01:20:07.590
because I'm trying to protect my skin

01:20:07.590 --> 01:20:09.380
from so many wrinkles and stuff,

01:20:09.380 --> 01:20:13.273
but also skin cancer is
somewhat of an issue as well.

01:20:14.580 --> 01:20:16.640
So, basically, the point is that vitamin D

01:20:16.640 --> 01:20:20.010
is a steroid hormone,
meaning it actually binds

01:20:20.010 --> 01:20:24.512
to a receptor and another
receptor dimerizes with it,

01:20:24.512 --> 01:20:26.190
the retinoid receptor,

01:20:26.190 --> 01:20:29.850
and that complex goes into
the nucleus of a cell,

01:20:29.850 --> 01:20:31.180
where your DNA is,

01:20:31.180 --> 01:20:34.280
and it recognizes little sequences of DNA

01:20:34.280 --> 01:20:35.940
called vitamin D response elements.

01:20:35.940 --> 01:20:37.410
They're called VDREs.

01:20:37.410 --> 01:20:40.910
They're specific sequences
of DNA that this complex,

01:20:40.910 --> 01:20:42.850
vitamin D bound with
the vitamin D receptor,

01:20:42.850 --> 01:20:44.440
goes inside and recognizes

01:20:44.440 --> 01:20:46.490
and turns on a whole host of genes,

01:20:46.490 --> 01:20:47.830
turns off a whole host of genes.

01:20:47.830 --> 01:20:51.520
I mean, this is important stuff.

01:20:51.520 --> 01:20:53.690
Imagine 70% of the population

01:20:53.690 --> 01:20:56.360
having insufficient testosterone, right?

01:20:56.360 --> 01:20:57.900
It's a steroid hormone.

01:20:57.900 --> 01:21:00.930
- We might be headed
there, but probably not.

01:21:00.930 --> 01:21:03.980
No, I think that names are very important,

01:21:03.980 --> 01:21:07.080
and I think that one of the
issues is that vitamin D

01:21:07.080 --> 01:21:09.330
is called vitamin D.

01:21:09.330 --> 01:21:13.730
It's not called DHEA or
variant blah, blah, blah.

01:21:13.730 --> 01:21:15.790
It doesn't sound like a hormone.

01:21:15.790 --> 01:21:20.140
I'm glad that you're mentioning
skin as the major interface

01:21:20.140 --> 01:21:22.520
between the environment
and vitamin D synthesis

01:21:22.520 --> 01:21:24.370
because a lot of people think of skin

01:21:24.370 --> 01:21:26.870
as just a protective sheath around us

01:21:26.870 --> 01:21:28.790
or something to adorn
ourselves with earrings

01:21:28.790 --> 01:21:30.140
or tattoos or whatever,

01:21:30.140 --> 01:21:32.420
but skin obviously serves those roles,

01:21:32.420 --> 01:21:34.950
but the skin is an endocrine organ.

01:21:34.950 --> 01:21:38.150
It has the capacity to make
things that impact hormones

01:21:38.150 --> 01:21:39.660
and to make hormones.

01:21:39.660 --> 01:21:42.700
There's this beautiful
study out this last year.

01:21:42.700 --> 01:21:44.170
This took place over in Israel,

01:21:44.170 --> 01:21:45.570
where they had people get outside

01:21:45.570 --> 01:21:49.010
for 20 or 30 minutes a
day, three times a week,

01:21:49.010 --> 01:21:52.360
exposing a culturally acceptable

01:21:52.360 --> 01:21:55.950
yet substantial amount of
their skin during that time,

01:21:55.950 --> 01:21:59.260
and saw big increases in
testosterone and estrogen.

01:21:59.260 --> 01:22:01.950
And this is through a
keratinocyte-linked pathway

01:22:01.950 --> 01:22:03.770
involving p53.

01:22:03.770 --> 01:22:04.660
This was done in humans,

01:22:04.660 --> 01:22:06.310
but they did some knockout
studies in parallel.

01:22:06.310 --> 01:22:08.380
And what this study told me or reminded me

01:22:08.380 --> 01:22:09.910
is that skin is an endocrine organ.

01:22:09.910 --> 01:22:12.430
So the idea that sun could
trigger the activation

01:22:12.430 --> 01:22:16.550
of production of a hormone
is really interesting

01:22:16.550 --> 01:22:17.770
and makes total sense.

01:22:17.770 --> 01:22:20.180
So, when vitamin D gets into cells,

01:22:20.180 --> 01:22:21.850
and it's binding to these VDR

01:22:22.934 --> 01:22:25.165
- [Rhonda] Es.
- Es,

01:22:25.165 --> 01:22:27.780
what sorts of things are they triggering?

01:22:27.780 --> 01:22:28.790
So, for testosterone,

01:22:28.790 --> 01:22:30.560
we know it's going to
trigger protein synthesis,

01:22:30.560 --> 01:22:32.300
muscle growth, tendon strength, et cetera,

01:22:32.300 --> 01:22:35.360
With estrogen, it's going
to keep your neurons going,

01:22:35.360 --> 01:22:36.710
your joints feeling good.

01:22:36.710 --> 01:22:39.000
I always remind people that, by the way,

01:22:39.000 --> 01:22:40.750
'cause guys always seem to want

01:22:40.750 --> 01:22:43.070
to increase their testosterone
and reduce their estrogen.

01:22:43.070 --> 01:22:45.800
Just remind people, if you
reduce your estrogen, guys,

01:22:45.800 --> 01:22:47.810
your libido will plummet to near zero.

01:22:47.810 --> 01:22:49.620
Don't crush your estrogen.

01:22:49.620 --> 01:22:50.970
It'll also make you stupid.

01:22:52.070 --> 01:22:54.913
If you're not already stupid,
it will make you stupid.

01:22:55.760 --> 01:22:58.900
So estrogen's vitally important
for males and females.

01:22:58.900 --> 01:23:01.470
When vitamin D gets into cells,

01:23:01.470 --> 01:23:04.131
what sorts of things is it stimulating?

01:23:04.131 --> 01:23:06.890
- Okay, so, first of all,
it's regulating more than 5%

01:23:06.890 --> 01:23:08.963
of the protein-encoded human genome.

01:23:10.770 --> 01:23:14.280
I say more than because, when
I was looking at this data

01:23:14.280 --> 01:23:19.280
really in depth starting in 2012 to 2014,

01:23:20.560 --> 01:23:22.780
it was that, and then it's now grown.

01:23:22.780 --> 01:23:25.750
But one of the important things
that you'll find interesting

01:23:25.750 --> 01:23:27.390
that I published on back in 2014

01:23:27.390 --> 01:23:32.390
was that I'd gone through
this big, published database,

01:23:32.590 --> 01:23:34.710
where someone had
published all these genes

01:23:34.710 --> 01:23:36.490
they found VDREs in,

01:23:36.490 --> 01:23:41.490
and basically I found that
tryptophan hydroxylase-1

01:23:42.170 --> 01:23:44.900
and tryptophan hydroxylase-2 was on there.

01:23:44.900 --> 01:23:46.330
And so then I started
looking at the sequence,

01:23:46.330 --> 01:23:48.090
and I was doing some in silico work,

01:23:48.090 --> 01:23:52.930
and it turns out that the VDREs
in tryptophan hydroxylase-2.

01:23:52.930 --> 01:23:56.810
So, for people listening,
tryptophan hydroxylase

01:23:56.810 --> 01:24:00.860
is an enzyme that converts
tryptophan into serotonin.

01:24:00.860 --> 01:24:05.050
So tryptophan is an amino acid
that we get from our food.

01:24:05.050 --> 01:24:09.500
You convert tryptophan
into serotonin in the gut,

01:24:09.500 --> 01:24:11.250
but you also do it in the brain.

01:24:11.250 --> 01:24:13.840
However, serotonin does not
cross the blood-brain barrier.

01:24:13.840 --> 01:24:16.550
So tryptophan has to get into your brain,

01:24:16.550 --> 01:24:19.102
and then you have to convert
it to serotonin in your brain.

01:24:19.102 --> 01:24:21.640
Well, the enzyme that
does that in your brain

01:24:21.640 --> 01:24:23.680
is called tryptophan hydroxylase-2,

01:24:23.680 --> 01:24:26.773
and it's activated by vitamin D.

01:24:26.773 --> 01:24:30.560
The one in the gut is actually
tryptophan hydroxylase-1.

01:24:30.560 --> 01:24:33.370
Some of my published work hypothesized

01:24:33.370 --> 01:24:35.820
that it might actually
be repressed by vitamin D

01:24:35.820 --> 01:24:37.690
because it has a sequence.

01:24:37.690 --> 01:24:41.580
The sequence itself, this
12 nucleotide sequence,

01:24:41.580 --> 01:24:43.960
can determine, to some degree,

01:24:43.960 --> 01:24:45.749
whether it's going to be
activated or turned off.

01:24:45.749 --> 01:24:47.580
And I was able to kind of
look at that and think,

01:24:47.580 --> 01:24:49.220
oh, maybe this and that.

01:24:49.220 --> 01:24:50.610
Since then, there have been some groups

01:24:50.610 --> 01:24:53.310
that have confirmed more with in vivo

01:24:53.310 --> 01:24:54.950
and/or in vitro studies.

01:24:54.950 --> 01:24:58.320
Mine was all in silico and
all that stuff, but anyways.

01:24:58.320 --> 01:25:00.673
So serotonin, a really important one.

01:25:01.942 --> 01:25:05.490
This is regulating our
immune cell, immune system.

01:25:05.490 --> 01:25:08.463
It's regulating our blood pressure.

01:25:09.980 --> 01:25:11.713
That's water retention.

01:25:12.580 --> 01:25:14.510
Bone, of course, homeostasis.

01:25:14.510 --> 01:25:16.460
5%. More than 5%.

01:25:16.460 --> 01:25:19.350
I can't tell you. Like, so much.

01:25:19.350 --> 01:25:23.613
- And with 70% of the
US population deficient,

01:25:24.910 --> 01:25:27.480
I'm beginning to think that
this could be the linchpin

01:25:27.480 --> 01:25:29.980
on a number of really important issues.

01:25:29.980 --> 01:25:34.303
So supplementing vitamin
D3 is what I normally hear.

01:25:35.170 --> 01:25:36.630
I do.

01:25:36.630 --> 01:25:39.470
I think I end up taking 5,000 IUs,

01:25:39.470 --> 01:25:42.340
sometimes 10 IUs of vitamin D3 per day.

01:25:42.340 --> 01:25:43.480
Just done that for a long time,

01:25:43.480 --> 01:25:45.830
and I've had my levels
tested and they're in range.

01:25:45.830 --> 01:25:48.160
But I have a family member,
I'll just mention this.

01:25:48.160 --> 01:25:51.030
I have a family member
who was not feeling well,

01:25:51.030 --> 01:25:53.030
just kind of feeling off, a little low,

01:25:53.030 --> 01:25:54.390
had some digestive issues,

01:25:54.390 --> 01:25:56.590
this went on a long period of time,

01:25:56.590 --> 01:26:01.360
was taking, on my
recommendation, 15,000 IUs of D3

01:26:01.360 --> 01:26:03.780
and was still deficient in D3.

01:26:03.780 --> 01:26:05.513
Now takes, and I'm not
suggesting anyone do this.

01:26:05.513 --> 01:26:07.820
This is a special case, perhaps,

01:26:07.820 --> 01:26:11.190
but no chronic illness
that we're aware of.

01:26:11.190 --> 01:26:13.810
Needs to take 30,000 IUs per day

01:26:13.810 --> 01:26:18.100
in order to bring their
D3 range just into normal,

01:26:18.100 --> 01:26:20.490
which, to me, is striking
because they eat quite well,

01:26:20.490 --> 01:26:22.710
they're a healthy weight, et cetera,

01:26:22.710 --> 01:26:24.070
and it's made a tremendous difference

01:26:24.070 --> 01:26:24.903
in terms of their mood.

01:26:24.903 --> 01:26:25.960
Now, of course, this is correlative.

01:26:25.960 --> 01:26:27.250
Now they feel better. They're doing it.

01:26:27.250 --> 01:26:29.330
Who knows? They're probably
also getting outside more.

01:26:29.330 --> 01:26:33.000
But I think people need to get tested.

01:26:33.000 --> 01:26:35.560
They need to get their D3 levels tested.

01:26:35.560 --> 01:26:38.670
But where and what is
a good starting range

01:26:38.670 --> 01:26:41.270
for people to think
about D3 supplementation,

01:26:41.270 --> 01:26:45.470
and, again, foods that can increase D3?

01:26:45.470 --> 01:26:49.290
- So vitamin D3 is a good
way to supplement with it.

01:26:49.290 --> 01:26:50.980
Vitamin D2 would be a plant source.

01:26:50.980 --> 01:26:53.550
You often find it fortified in foods

01:26:53.550 --> 01:26:55.220
like milk, usually D2.

01:26:55.220 --> 01:26:58.163
- Does anyone still
drink milk besides kids?

01:26:59.910 --> 01:27:02.270
- Out here, it's like
you can't find cows milk.

01:27:02.270 --> 01:27:03.538
- [Rhonda] I mean, all the lattes

01:27:03.538 --> 01:27:05.303
that you're getting.
- Oat milk, soy milk.

01:27:05.303 --> 01:27:06.310
What's the other one?

01:27:06.310 --> 01:27:07.880
- [Rhonda] They're
fortified in those as well.

01:27:07.880 --> 01:27:08.713
- Oh, they are. Okay.

01:27:08.713 --> 01:27:09.760
- [Rhonda] They are, yeah.
They're fortified in-

01:27:09.760 --> 01:27:10.593
- I have a hard time

01:27:10.593 --> 01:27:11.610
finding cows milk.
- Almond milk and oat milk

01:27:11.610 --> 01:27:13.480
and all that stuff, yeah.

01:27:13.480 --> 01:27:15.620
They're in all that stuff.

01:27:15.620 --> 01:27:19.720
Vitamin D is naturally to
some degree in fatty fish.

01:27:19.720 --> 01:27:22.330
You think about cod liver oil, right?

01:27:22.330 --> 01:27:23.163
It has vitamin D,

01:27:23.163 --> 01:27:26.536
but you're not going
to correct a deficiency

01:27:26.536 --> 01:27:29.450
with eating fish for your vitamin D.

01:27:29.450 --> 01:27:31.920
You're either going to
correct it with sun exposure,

01:27:31.920 --> 01:27:34.260
being in the right area,
having the right amount of sun,

01:27:34.260 --> 01:27:35.900
and being the right age,

01:27:35.900 --> 01:27:38.470
because, as you get old,
you become very inefficient

01:27:38.470 --> 01:27:43.470
at converting vitamin D,
making vitamin D3 in your skin.

01:27:43.737 --> 01:27:45.447
- Well, that's probably
what was going on here

01:27:45.447 --> 01:27:47.183
'cause this person is
getting up in their age.

01:27:47.183 --> 01:27:50.435
- There's a lot of single
nucleotide polymorphisms.

01:27:50.435 --> 01:27:52.100
We talked about APOE4 before previously,

01:27:52.100 --> 01:27:56.270
but there's a variety of
genes that people have,

01:27:56.270 --> 01:27:57.460
very common, actually.

01:27:57.460 --> 01:27:59.840
In fact, I've had many
people that have done

01:27:59.840 --> 01:28:01.080
that exact same thing.

01:28:01.080 --> 01:28:04.510
So measuring your vitamin D levels

01:28:04.510 --> 01:28:06.430
before and after supplementation

01:28:06.430 --> 01:28:08.400
is the only way you're going
to figure that out, right?

01:28:08.400 --> 01:28:09.233
Very important.

01:28:09.233 --> 01:28:11.230
If you don't measure it, you don't know.

01:28:11.230 --> 01:28:13.363
You can't know what you don't measure.

01:28:14.890 --> 01:28:16.610
So there's a variety of SNPs

01:28:16.610 --> 01:28:19.150
that basically make that
conversion inefficient.

01:28:19.150 --> 01:28:21.462
And, in fact, there've been a lot

01:28:21.462 --> 01:28:23.390
of these Mendelian randomization studies.

01:28:23.390 --> 01:28:27.140
So these are studies where scientists

01:28:27.140 --> 01:28:30.080
will look at commons SNPs,

01:28:30.080 --> 01:28:33.310
people that have these
common variations of a gene.

01:28:33.310 --> 01:28:35.850
That's more than 1% of the population.

01:28:35.850 --> 01:28:37.240
So it's not a random mutation.

01:28:37.240 --> 01:28:40.777
It's actually found in a sizeable
percent of the population.

01:28:40.777 --> 01:28:43.410
And then, they've looked
at various outcomes.

01:28:43.410 --> 01:28:45.360
And a lot of times, they'll look at genes

01:28:45.360 --> 01:28:47.890
that are also involved in
some kind of lifestyle factor,

01:28:47.890 --> 01:28:48.850
so vitamin D,

01:28:48.850 --> 01:28:52.000
and SNPs that basically
make the conversion

01:28:52.000 --> 01:28:55.470
of either vitamin D precursor into D3,

01:28:55.470 --> 01:28:58.210
or D3 into 25-hydroxy vitamin D,

01:28:58.210 --> 01:28:59.810
or into the active steroid hormone,

01:28:59.810 --> 01:29:02.130
which is 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3.

01:29:02.130 --> 01:29:04.630
And there's a variety of
different SNPs that show.

01:29:05.590 --> 01:29:07.680
So you're not looking at
vitamin D levels at all.

01:29:07.680 --> 01:29:08.900
You're looking at just the SNPs.

01:29:08.900 --> 01:29:11.510
And you know, if they have
it, they have low vitamin D.

01:29:11.510 --> 01:29:14.480
So it's really a way of doing

01:29:14.480 --> 01:29:17.340
a beautifully randomized controlled trial

01:29:17.340 --> 01:29:22.240
with an observational study
because you're not biased.

01:29:22.240 --> 01:29:24.480
Vitamin D levels are also
associated with health.

01:29:24.480 --> 01:29:26.430
People that have higher vitamin D

01:29:26.430 --> 01:29:27.263
are either outside more,

01:29:27.263 --> 01:29:28.690
they're more physically active,

01:29:28.690 --> 01:29:30.390
or they're aware of their health

01:29:30.390 --> 01:29:31.430
and their supplementing, right?

01:29:31.430 --> 01:29:33.560
So you always have to worry about that

01:29:33.560 --> 01:29:35.720
when you're doing an observational study.

01:29:35.720 --> 01:29:37.730
Dealing in randomization is
beautiful for that reason,

01:29:37.730 --> 01:29:39.410
where you're now just random.

01:29:39.410 --> 01:29:41.340
People randomly have these genes,

01:29:41.340 --> 01:29:43.880
and there's no health status.

01:29:43.880 --> 01:29:45.630
If you have the SNP, like your friend,

01:29:45.630 --> 01:29:47.840
like your family member
was healthy and all.

01:29:47.840 --> 01:29:48.673
They were healthy,

01:29:48.673 --> 01:29:50.690
and yet they couldn't get
their D levels up, right?

01:29:50.690 --> 01:29:53.630
So these Mendelian
randomization studies have found

01:29:53.630 --> 01:29:57.950
that people that can't
convert into the precursor,

01:29:57.950 --> 01:30:00.380
the 25-hydroxy vitamin D, which
is usually what's measured.

01:30:00.380 --> 01:30:03.500
It's the most stable form
of vitamin D in the body.

01:30:03.500 --> 01:30:06.240
They have a higher all-cause
mortality, if they can't do it.

01:30:06.240 --> 01:30:08.010
So people that don't have it

01:30:08.010 --> 01:30:09.540
have a lower all-cause mortality.

01:30:09.540 --> 01:30:12.890
They have a higher
respiratory-related mortality.

01:30:12.890 --> 01:30:15.110
They have a higher
cancer-related mortality.

01:30:15.110 --> 01:30:19.190
So, to me... Now, why
did I get on this rant?

01:30:19.190 --> 01:30:21.070
Oh, because your family member.

01:30:21.070 --> 01:30:23.440
So, basically, they also are more likely

01:30:23.440 --> 01:30:24.460
to get multiple sclerosis.

01:30:24.460 --> 01:30:26.501
This has all been done with
Mendelian randomization.

01:30:26.501 --> 01:30:29.550
And so it really does
hammer home the importance

01:30:29.550 --> 01:30:31.670
of measuring your vitamin D levels

01:30:31.670 --> 01:30:34.643
and being very proactive about that.

01:30:34.643 --> 01:30:36.770
I mean, you can get it done anywhere.

01:30:36.770 --> 01:30:39.130
Your doctor will do it. You
ask 'em to do it, you know.

01:30:39.130 --> 01:30:40.763
So supplementation wise,

01:30:41.660 --> 01:30:44.210
typically, if you don't
have one of those SNPs,

01:30:44.210 --> 01:30:47.840
for the most part, taking
1,000 IUs of vitamin D

01:30:47.840 --> 01:30:49.430
will raise blood levels

01:30:49.430 --> 01:30:51.960
by around five nanograms per milliliter.

01:30:51.960 --> 01:30:53.150
So let's say you're deficient,

01:30:53.150 --> 01:30:55.180
you're 20 nanograms per milliliter,

01:30:55.180 --> 01:30:57.030
and you want to get to 40.

01:30:57.030 --> 01:30:58.990
You're going to need at least 4,000 IUs,

01:30:58.990 --> 01:31:02.550
if you are normal and don't
have any of these SNPs

01:31:02.550 --> 01:31:04.390
that change your metabolism
of vitamin D, right?

01:31:04.390 --> 01:31:08.200
- Does it matter when you take
it relative to sun exposure,

01:31:08.200 --> 01:31:11.120
time of day, with or without food?

01:31:11.120 --> 01:31:15.830
- I've seen some not so
great preliminary evidence

01:31:15.830 --> 01:31:18.870
suggesting maybe time of day is important.

01:31:18.870 --> 01:31:20.100
I don't think it.

01:31:20.100 --> 01:31:24.753
Like, I can't seem to find
anything that really suggests.

01:31:24.753 --> 01:31:27.260
Because for it to actually be
converted into the hormone,

01:31:27.260 --> 01:31:28.755
I mean, it's stored.

01:31:28.755 --> 01:31:29.588
- It's slow-acting.

01:31:29.588 --> 01:31:31.040
These steroid hormones are slow-acting.

01:31:31.040 --> 01:31:33.010
- Yeah, it's not like immediate thing.

01:31:33.010 --> 01:31:36.980
So maybe we'll get some new
data that's like otherwise,

01:31:36.980 --> 01:31:38.430
but I just don't, yeah.

01:31:38.430 --> 01:31:40.260
- It simplifies the problem anyway.

01:31:40.260 --> 01:31:42.490
So, for people who are
going to be stubborn

01:31:42.490 --> 01:31:46.220
and not get their D3 levels
tested or their D levels tested,

01:31:46.220 --> 01:31:50.840
and simply say, "Oh,
I'll just take some D3."

01:31:50.840 --> 01:31:55.040
That was me, by the
way, until I got tested.

01:31:55.040 --> 01:31:57.360
I threw 5,000 IUs into
the mix and figured,

01:31:57.360 --> 01:31:58.260
well, it's not going to kill me.

01:31:58.260 --> 01:32:00.140
It'll bring my vitamin D levels up.

01:32:00.140 --> 01:32:03.480
I realize that's a bit of a
coarse way to approach it,

01:32:03.480 --> 01:32:06.710
but I feel fine, and I'm still
breathing and ambulatory.

01:32:06.710 --> 01:32:09.373
So, is that reasonable?

01:32:09.373 --> 01:32:12.600
1,000 to 5,000 IUs for most
people will be reasonably safe.

01:32:12.600 --> 01:32:16.490
Again, just assuming that people
are going to just jump to it

01:32:16.490 --> 01:32:17.840
without the blood test.

01:32:17.840 --> 01:32:22.810
- Of course, I think that,
if we look at the literature,

01:32:22.810 --> 01:32:24.500
the scientific literature,

01:32:24.500 --> 01:32:27.840
it is extremely hard to get hypercalcemia,

01:32:27.840 --> 01:32:29.210
which would be the major concern

01:32:29.210 --> 01:32:32.350
with really high levels of
vitamin D3 supplementation.

01:32:32.350 --> 01:32:34.810
I mean, we're talking
like hundreds of thousands

01:32:34.810 --> 01:32:38.204
of IU a day for a long time.

01:32:38.204 --> 01:32:40.700
- Hundreds of thousands.
- Yes, yes.

01:32:40.700 --> 01:32:43.440
Now, the upper tolerable intake was set

01:32:43.440 --> 01:32:48.440
by the Medicine Institute to be 4,000.

01:32:48.551 --> 01:32:52.301
It was kind of like one of
those things where it's safe.

01:32:52.301 --> 01:32:55.725
I personally take 5,000 IUs a day as well,

01:32:55.725 --> 01:33:00.093
and my levels really hover
around 50 nanograms per mill.

01:33:01.486 --> 01:33:04.690
I don't put sunscreen on all the time.

01:33:04.690 --> 01:33:05.897
I do put it on my face and I wear a hat,

01:33:05.897 --> 01:33:07.600
but some of my skin is being exposed,

01:33:07.600 --> 01:33:10.850
so I do make it from the sun as well.

01:33:10.850 --> 01:33:13.260
- I'm glad you brought up the fact

01:33:13.260 --> 01:33:15.270
that you keep arms exposed.

01:33:15.270 --> 01:33:18.330
Because, in these studies
that I mentioned before,

01:33:18.330 --> 01:33:20.280
looking at sun exposure on skin

01:33:20.280 --> 01:33:22.490
and increases in other hormones,

01:33:22.490 --> 01:33:24.340
testosterone, estrogen, mainly,

01:33:24.340 --> 01:33:26.820
it became clear from looking at those data

01:33:26.820 --> 01:33:29.980
that the amount of skin that
you expose is important,

01:33:29.980 --> 01:33:31.680
which makes perfect
sense once you hear that,

01:33:31.680 --> 01:33:32.590
but I think most people are thinking,

01:33:32.590 --> 01:33:34.240
oh, I'm out in the sun,

01:33:34.240 --> 01:33:37.050
but are you wearing shorts and a t-shirt,

01:33:37.050 --> 01:33:39.759
or are you wearing a
sweatshirt and it's a hoodie?

01:33:39.759 --> 01:33:41.470
Are you all covered up out in the sun?

01:33:41.470 --> 01:33:43.640
Well, that might be great for
setting your circadian rhythm

01:33:43.640 --> 01:33:45.460
by way of light through the eyes

01:33:45.460 --> 01:33:47.360
'cause that's the primary
mechanism for that.

01:33:47.360 --> 01:33:51.060
But seems to me that the
more of your body surface

01:33:51.060 --> 01:33:54.170
that you can safely and appropriately,

01:33:54.170 --> 01:33:55.003
please, folks,

01:33:55.003 --> 01:33:57.320
appropriately expose to the sun,

01:33:57.320 --> 01:33:59.670
the more vitamin D you're going to create.

01:33:59.670 --> 01:34:03.810
So laying out on your back
deck in shorts and a t-shirt

01:34:03.810 --> 01:34:05.370
with arms exposed and legs exposed

01:34:05.370 --> 01:34:07.410
is a very different stimulus

01:34:07.410 --> 01:34:10.200
than walking around in
jeans and a sweatshirt.

01:34:10.200 --> 01:34:12.400
- Absolutely.
- Right? Okay, okay.

01:34:12.400 --> 01:34:13.870
Especially if you have
sunscreen on your face.

01:34:13.870 --> 01:34:16.020
I know it almost seems
like trivially simple,

01:34:16.020 --> 01:34:17.750
but I'm not sure that
people are used to thinking

01:34:17.750 --> 01:34:22.410
about their skin as a interface
to create these hormones.

01:34:22.410 --> 01:34:24.450
- Yeah.
- So surface area matters.

01:34:24.450 --> 01:34:29.050
- And by the way, there have
been studies looking at people

01:34:29.050 --> 01:34:31.450
that are deficient in vitamin D.

01:34:31.450 --> 01:34:33.330
In this case, it was African Americans

01:34:33.330 --> 01:34:36.500
that were given a 4,000 IU
a day vitamin D supplement

01:34:36.500 --> 01:34:38.693
to bring them back to sufficient levels.

01:34:39.890 --> 01:34:43.330
This was a smaller
study than I would like,

01:34:43.330 --> 01:34:47.919
but it reversed their
epigenetic aging by three years.

01:34:47.919 --> 01:34:51.150
Because, again, it's a hormone.

01:34:51.150 --> 01:34:52.530
It's regulating more than 5%

01:34:52.530 --> 01:34:55.181
of your protein-encoding human genome.

01:34:55.181 --> 01:34:56.520
There's been studies looking

01:34:56.520 --> 01:34:59.750
at vitamin D receptor knockout mice.

01:34:59.750 --> 01:35:01.680
And I use this a lot in my presentations

01:35:01.680 --> 01:35:04.480
when I'm talking about
vitamin D and longevity.

01:35:04.480 --> 01:35:05.760
If you look at these animals,

01:35:05.760 --> 01:35:08.700
the vitamin D receptor,
as I mentioned earlier,

01:35:08.700 --> 01:35:10.480
vitamin D binds to the receptor,

01:35:10.480 --> 01:35:12.530
and then it complexes with
the retinoid receptor,

01:35:12.530 --> 01:35:14.050
and they go into the nucleus as a complex

01:35:14.050 --> 01:35:16.120
and turn on and turn off genes.

01:35:16.120 --> 01:35:17.410
Well, if you get rid of that receptor,

01:35:17.410 --> 01:35:19.573
which is what you can
do in animal studies,

01:35:20.439 --> 01:35:23.570
you can determine what
effects there will be

01:35:23.570 --> 01:35:24.790
with no vitamin D.

01:35:24.790 --> 01:35:26.767
Like, how do you study no vitamin D?

01:35:26.767 --> 01:35:30.940
And so what was found
was that these animals,

01:35:30.940 --> 01:35:34.040
and, in fact, I don't think
it was a complete knockout

01:35:34.040 --> 01:35:36.243
'cause I think it might
be embryonic lethal, but-

01:35:36.243 --> 01:35:37.690
- [Andrew] Some hypomorph.
- Yes.

01:35:37.690 --> 01:35:40.360
- Which is basically geek speak for a gene

01:35:40.360 --> 01:35:44.760
is vastly reduced in its
function, number and function,

01:35:44.760 --> 01:35:46.290
number, people know what I mean,

01:35:46.290 --> 01:35:48.890
but isn't eliminated completely.

01:35:48.890 --> 01:35:50.370
- Right, well, these animals,

01:35:50.370 --> 01:35:53.450
if you look at them after
the age of four months,

01:35:53.450 --> 01:35:56.500
I mean, the mice look like.

01:35:56.500 --> 01:35:58.230
I mean, they're accelerated aging.

01:35:58.230 --> 01:36:00.243
They're wrinkled. They have no hair.

01:36:01.100 --> 01:36:02.530
Their lifespan's shorter.

01:36:02.530 --> 01:36:04.540
I mean, you can look at this animal

01:36:04.540 --> 01:36:06.283
and not know anything about
mice or work with them

01:36:06.283 --> 01:36:10.140
and be like, "That animal
looks like it's..."

01:36:10.140 --> 01:36:12.078
Of course, mice lifespans
are only like two,

01:36:12.078 --> 01:36:14.860
two and a half years,
but, like, 500 years old.

01:36:14.860 --> 01:36:16.850
- Looks like it went to
graduate school twice.

01:36:16.850 --> 01:36:17.700
- [Rhonda] Yeah.

01:36:17.700 --> 01:36:19.210
- Actually, graduate
school's a lot of fun.

01:36:19.210 --> 01:36:21.110
I like to think I aged
backwards in graduate school,

01:36:21.110 --> 01:36:21.943
which is not true.

01:36:21.943 --> 01:36:24.180
I look at the photos. I
definitely aged forward.

01:36:24.180 --> 01:36:25.013
You, on the other hand,

01:36:25.013 --> 01:36:27.250
look exactly the same
way you did 10 years ago.

01:36:27.250 --> 01:36:28.650
I'm not saying that to flatter you,

01:36:28.650 --> 01:36:29.700
but it's absolutely true.

01:36:29.700 --> 01:36:32.340
I mean, the data or the
data. It's remarkable.

01:36:32.340 --> 01:36:34.330
So I'm definitely going to try

01:36:34.330 --> 01:36:36.550
and get my omega-3 percentage up there.

01:36:36.550 --> 01:36:38.570
I'm not going to hinge it all on that,

01:36:38.570 --> 01:36:40.920
but clearly you're doing
a lot of things right.

01:36:41.930 --> 01:36:44.213
So, if I'm taking vitamin D3,

01:36:45.970 --> 01:36:47.970
I still need to get out into the sun.

01:36:47.970 --> 01:36:48.803
Correct?
- Absolutely.

01:36:48.803 --> 01:36:50.880
- Okay, I think a lot of
people don't know that,

01:36:50.880 --> 01:36:52.210
or, at least, I have family members

01:36:52.210 --> 01:36:53.390
that have been a little bit resistant.

01:36:53.390 --> 01:36:54.327
Like, "I take my vitamin D

01:36:54.327 --> 01:36:56.440
"so I don't need to get outside as much."

01:36:56.440 --> 01:36:58.690
I think people are really afraid
of getting out into the sun

01:36:58.690 --> 01:37:00.760
because they're worried about melanomas.

01:37:00.760 --> 01:37:04.900
And, to be honest, I'm
as scared of sunscreen

01:37:04.900 --> 01:37:06.510
as I am of melanoma.

01:37:06.510 --> 01:37:09.460
Some of the things in
sunscreen are really spooky,

01:37:09.460 --> 01:37:10.850
mainly the compound.

01:37:10.850 --> 01:37:12.620
And here, I'm not one of these conspiracy.

01:37:12.620 --> 01:37:14.520
I drink tap water. Listen, folks.

01:37:14.520 --> 01:37:16.950
People cringe, but I drink tap water.

01:37:16.950 --> 01:37:19.000
I have the occasional croissant or donut.

01:37:20.134 --> 01:37:21.700
90%, 80% of the time,

01:37:21.700 --> 01:37:23.600
I'm doing the right things,
the right way, I think.

01:37:23.600 --> 01:37:24.930
Although, I'm now going to improve on them

01:37:24.930 --> 01:37:26.130
with this new knowledge.

01:37:27.814 --> 01:37:30.870
But I don't like what I
see in most sunscreens.

01:37:30.870 --> 01:37:32.850
Because, if you look at these compounds,

01:37:32.850 --> 01:37:35.060
they cross the blood-brain barrier.

01:37:35.060 --> 01:37:38.340
I don't want compounds crossing
the blood-brain barrier.

01:37:38.340 --> 01:37:39.220
- Titanium dioxide?

01:37:39.220 --> 01:37:40.960
- Dioxide, some of the triclosans

01:37:40.960 --> 01:37:42.680
that are also in these cleansers.

01:37:42.680 --> 01:37:46.410
I mean, once you know a
little bit about neurons,

01:37:46.410 --> 01:37:48.240
folks, you realize the neurons you got

01:37:48.240 --> 01:37:51.170
are basically the ones you've
got for your entire life.

01:37:51.170 --> 01:37:52.770
There's a reason why there's
a blood-brain barrier,

01:37:52.770 --> 01:37:55.010
a blood-ovary, and a blood-testes barrier.

01:37:55.010 --> 01:37:56.640
It's because the genetic material resides

01:37:56.640 --> 01:37:58.380
in the testes, the ovaries, and the brain.

01:37:58.380 --> 01:37:59.500
Those neurons don't turn over.

01:37:59.500 --> 01:38:00.600
There are a few new neurons

01:38:00.600 --> 01:38:03.490
but not that many unless
you're a mouse, frankly.

01:38:03.490 --> 01:38:05.260
And so protecting those is very key,

01:38:05.260 --> 01:38:07.220
and a lot of the things in sunscreen

01:38:08.410 --> 01:38:10.080
are downright dangerous.

01:38:10.080 --> 01:38:12.660
So I think there are
sunscreens that are safe,

01:38:12.660 --> 01:38:14.730
but it's very hard to figure out

01:38:14.730 --> 01:38:17.870
which sunscreens are
free of these compounds.

01:38:17.870 --> 01:38:20.530
I'm amazed that they're
still on the market, frankly.

01:38:20.530 --> 01:38:24.750
- I've always geared towards
the ones with the minerals

01:38:24.750 --> 01:38:26.596
that are reflecting it.

01:38:26.596 --> 01:38:29.610
It is somewhat difficult
to penetrate things

01:38:29.610 --> 01:38:33.370
all the way through the skin
and get into the bloodstream,

01:38:33.370 --> 01:38:34.203
but I don't know.

01:38:34.203 --> 01:38:35.820
Maybe some of these compounds
get in there easily.

01:38:35.820 --> 01:38:38.320
I have seen the evidence
with some of those things.

01:38:38.320 --> 01:38:41.670
- Yeah, there is some
evidence they go transdermal.

01:38:41.670 --> 01:38:42.503
- And they get in.

01:38:42.503 --> 01:38:45.840
Okay, well, I know that some
of them react with the sun

01:38:45.840 --> 01:38:49.110
and, while they do protect
from the UVA and/or B,

01:38:49.110 --> 01:38:53.160
they form massive reactive
oxygen species and carcinogen.

01:38:53.160 --> 01:38:54.630
I mean, it's like the
very thing you're trying

01:38:54.630 --> 01:38:57.810
to protect yourself from
might actually cause-

01:38:57.810 --> 01:38:58.643
- [Andrew] Right.

01:38:58.643 --> 01:39:01.193
- We don't know. It's
completely speculation.

01:39:02.340 --> 01:39:04.650
There is, I think, some more
and more evidence coming out

01:39:04.650 --> 01:39:05.860
with some of those compounds.

01:39:05.860 --> 01:39:08.260
I can't remember all of
'em off the top of my head,

01:39:08.260 --> 01:39:11.093
but a lot of high-end ones also have.

01:39:12.060 --> 01:39:13.655
It's the chemical sunscreen ones.

01:39:13.655 --> 01:39:15.620
- Right, right.
- [Rhonda] The chemical ones.

01:39:15.620 --> 01:39:17.360
- I'm proposing that we do a journal club.

01:39:17.360 --> 01:39:19.570
A journal club, folks, is
where academics get together.

01:39:19.570 --> 01:39:21.960
Well, they read papers,
then they get together,

01:39:21.960 --> 01:39:23.670
and they pick apart the papers.

01:39:23.670 --> 01:39:25.250
There's a strong correlation

01:39:25.250 --> 01:39:27.110
between being an early graduate student

01:39:27.110 --> 01:39:28.200
and being the most critical.

01:39:28.200 --> 01:39:29.630
'Cause once you've actually
published some papers,

01:39:29.630 --> 01:39:33.340
you realize that, most studies,
people are doing their best

01:39:33.340 --> 01:39:34.980
within the context of what they can do.

01:39:34.980 --> 01:39:36.520
But it'd be great to do a journal club

01:39:36.520 --> 01:39:38.880
at some point about sunscreens

01:39:38.880 --> 01:39:40.190
'cause I'd love to really figure out

01:39:40.190 --> 01:39:41.660
what's in these compounds.

01:39:41.660 --> 01:39:43.140
People are using them like crazy.

01:39:43.140 --> 01:39:44.480
And I'm not one of
these people who's like,

01:39:44.480 --> 01:39:46.170
oh, I won't use commercial toothpaste

01:39:46.170 --> 01:39:47.930
or anything like that.

01:39:47.930 --> 01:39:49.240
Like I said, I drink tap water.

01:39:49.240 --> 01:39:50.800
I use commercial toothpaste, whatever.

01:39:50.800 --> 01:39:52.700
But when it comes to
sunscreen, it freaks me out

01:39:52.700 --> 01:39:55.740
because some of these
compounds do go transdermal

01:39:55.740 --> 01:39:57.930
and some of them cross
the blood-brain barrier,

01:39:57.930 --> 01:40:00.770
and I'd like to keep my
neurons free of that stuff.

01:40:00.770 --> 01:40:02.515
Anyway, we're speculating now.

01:40:02.515 --> 01:40:04.960
- Wear a hat.
- Wear a hat,

01:40:04.960 --> 01:40:08.120
but get out in the sun
and get your D3 levels up.

01:40:08.120 --> 01:40:10.650
Okay, so, we talked about
these plant-based compounds,

01:40:10.650 --> 01:40:13.270
the omega-3s, and D3.

01:40:13.270 --> 01:40:14.490
Unless there's something else

01:40:14.490 --> 01:40:18.220
that you just absolutely
must throw into the mix,

01:40:18.220 --> 01:40:21.070
I probably will return
us to the conversation

01:40:21.070 --> 01:40:23.120
that I opened up with, which
was about cold and heat,

01:40:23.120 --> 01:40:25.900
which, admittedly, I
pulled us off that path,

01:40:25.900 --> 01:40:28.370
so I take full responsibility for that.

01:40:28.370 --> 01:40:29.790
But before I do that,

01:40:29.790 --> 01:40:31.740
I just want to offer you the opportunity.

01:40:33.300 --> 01:40:36.720
Is there anything to supplement-based

01:40:36.720 --> 01:40:41.720
or food-based compounds that
you think are especially useful

01:40:42.210 --> 01:40:43.323
for brain and/or body health?

01:40:43.323 --> 01:40:46.569
- I do think magnesium is
important in there as well.

01:40:46.569 --> 01:40:51.010
I mean, I think, again, about
40% of the US population

01:40:51.010 --> 01:40:52.550
doesn't get enough magnesium.

01:40:52.550 --> 01:40:54.430
It's an essential mineral

01:40:54.430 --> 01:40:56.359
we're supposed to be
getting from our diet.

01:40:56.359 --> 01:40:57.353
- Involved in everything.

01:40:57.353 --> 01:41:01.290
- It is. It's also involved
in vitamin D metabolism.

01:41:01.290 --> 01:41:04.410
And, in fact, being deficient in magnesium

01:41:04.410 --> 01:41:05.990
may make it more difficult

01:41:05.990 --> 01:41:09.730
for you to actually
make vitamin D hormone,

01:41:09.730 --> 01:41:12.020
so that 1,25-dihyroxyvitamin D.

01:41:12.020 --> 01:41:14.250
So one of those other factors, again,

01:41:14.250 --> 01:41:15.500
we talked about genetics,

01:41:15.500 --> 01:41:17.650
but there's also magnesium status as well.

01:41:17.650 --> 01:41:19.593
Considering 40%, that's a big number.

01:41:20.670 --> 01:41:25.670
Now, magnesium's also
involved in making ATP,

01:41:26.260 --> 01:41:29.360
the energetic currency of our cells.

01:41:29.360 --> 01:41:31.960
Basically, all of our cells
need ATP to do anything.

01:41:33.020 --> 01:41:35.250
It's also involved in utilizing ATP

01:41:35.250 --> 01:41:36.880
as well as DNA repair enzymes.

01:41:36.880 --> 01:41:38.110
These are enzymes that are involved

01:41:38.110 --> 01:41:39.980
in repairing damage to our DNA.

01:41:39.980 --> 01:41:43.470
I personally think that
magnesium insufficiency

01:41:45.395 --> 01:41:48.220
causes an insidious type of damage daily

01:41:48.220 --> 01:41:50.620
that you can't look in the mirror and see.

01:41:50.620 --> 01:41:53.210
When you're deficient in vitamin C,

01:41:53.210 --> 01:41:55.337
you're like, "My gums are falling apart.

01:41:55.337 --> 01:41:56.600
"I have scurvy, right?"

01:41:56.600 --> 01:41:58.520
But you can't see DNA damage.

01:41:58.520 --> 01:41:59.600
You can't see it, but it's happening.

01:41:59.600 --> 01:42:00.637
It's happening right now in my body,

01:42:00.637 --> 01:42:01.720
and it's happening in your body.

01:42:01.720 --> 01:42:05.690
It's happening. Normal metabolism
is happening every day.

01:42:05.690 --> 01:42:07.100
But we repair that damage.

01:42:07.100 --> 01:42:09.180
We have repair enzymes in our body

01:42:09.180 --> 01:42:10.810
called DNA repair enzymes.

01:42:10.810 --> 01:42:12.220
They require magnesium.

01:42:12.220 --> 01:42:15.200
Magnesium is a co-factor for them.

01:42:15.200 --> 01:42:17.370
What that means, a co-factor means,

01:42:17.370 --> 01:42:19.640
enzymes need it to function properly,

01:42:19.640 --> 01:42:22.680
and so, without that co-factor,

01:42:22.680 --> 01:42:24.180
they're not doing it properly.

01:42:25.330 --> 01:42:27.660
The way I like to think
about magnesium. It's easy.

01:42:27.660 --> 01:42:29.230
'Cause people go, "What
food should I eat?"

01:42:29.230 --> 01:42:30.740
Naturally, that's the next question.

01:42:30.740 --> 01:42:34.130
Well, magnesium is at the center
of a chlorophyll molecule.

01:42:34.130 --> 01:42:36.690
Chlorophyll is what gives
plants their green color.

01:42:36.690 --> 01:42:40.513
So dark, leafy greens
are high in magnesium.

01:42:41.682 --> 01:42:44.169
Basically, what does the 40%

01:42:44.169 --> 01:42:46.600
insufficiency in the US tell us?

01:42:46.600 --> 01:42:47.790
People aren't eating their greens.

01:42:47.790 --> 01:42:48.730
They're not eating their greens.

01:42:48.730 --> 01:42:49.740
They're eating their packaged food.

01:42:49.740 --> 01:42:50.573
They're eating their processed food.

01:42:50.573 --> 01:42:52.440
The standard American
diet isn't really high

01:42:52.440 --> 01:42:54.172
in dark, leafy greens.

01:42:54.172 --> 01:42:57.573
So dark, leafy greens are how
I like to get my magnesium.

01:42:57.573 --> 01:43:00.620
I think it comes along with
all these other important.

01:43:00.620 --> 01:43:03.710
I mean, you get calcium in
them. You get vitamin K1.

01:43:03.710 --> 01:43:05.260
You're getting a lot of
other micronutrients,

01:43:05.260 --> 01:43:07.590
and you're getting other compounds
that we don't know about,

01:43:07.590 --> 01:43:10.433
and ones that we know about,
like sulforaphane, right?

01:43:12.370 --> 01:43:13.603
- As with broccoli,

01:43:14.610 --> 01:43:18.050
do I need to eat the
dark, leafy greens raw?

01:43:18.050 --> 01:43:19.910
And, in this case, I'm
a little more open to it

01:43:19.910 --> 01:43:22.670
because I actually like the
taste of, dare I say, kale.

01:43:22.670 --> 01:43:25.290
And kale's a dark, leafy green, right?

01:43:25.290 --> 01:43:28.425
- Yes, and it's high in
lutein and zeaxanthin as well.

01:43:28.425 --> 01:43:30.430
- I'm a trichromat, meaning
I'm not color blind,

01:43:30.430 --> 01:43:31.990
but I just want to make sure it falls

01:43:31.990 --> 01:43:33.090
under the strict category.

01:43:33.090 --> 01:43:34.027
'Cause every once in a while, I'm like,

01:43:34.027 --> 01:43:34.990
"Oh, I eat my vegetables."

01:43:34.990 --> 01:43:35.823
I like avocados,

01:43:35.823 --> 01:43:37.720
and people remind me
avocados are not a vegetable.

01:43:37.720 --> 01:43:39.820
I love vegetables also.

01:43:39.820 --> 01:43:42.100
So kale, what are some other examples?

01:43:42.100 --> 01:43:45.900
- Kale, spinach, chard, like
Swiss chard, rainbow chard,

01:43:45.900 --> 01:43:47.150
romaine lettuce.

01:43:47.150 --> 01:43:49.327
- Is the bitterness an
important component to this?

01:43:49.327 --> 01:43:52.573
- For magnesium, no, but for sulforaphane,

01:43:53.690 --> 01:43:54.800
for cruciferous vegetables.

01:43:54.800 --> 01:43:58.680
That would be the brassica family.

01:43:58.680 --> 01:44:02.020
But your question about cooking them.

01:44:02.020 --> 01:44:05.637
So magnesium, it is
bound to the food matrix,

01:44:05.637 --> 01:44:10.613
and it can be somewhat less bioavailable.

01:44:12.463 --> 01:44:17.140
So cooking it can somewhat
release the magnesium,

01:44:17.140 --> 01:44:18.890
but it goes into the water too.

01:44:18.890 --> 01:44:21.650
So you have to either steam it

01:44:21.650 --> 01:44:24.630
or kind of like get your water in.

01:44:24.630 --> 01:44:26.030
- You can drink the water.

01:44:26.030 --> 01:44:28.810
- Yeah, I personally don't worry about it.

01:44:28.810 --> 01:44:30.280
- Okay, great.
- [Rhonda] I just don't worry.

01:44:30.280 --> 01:44:31.730
- Well, if you don't worry,
I'm not going to worry.

01:44:31.730 --> 01:44:34.290
- But I also do supplement with magnesium.

01:44:34.290 --> 01:44:37.270
So supplementation with magnesium,

01:44:37.270 --> 01:44:39.150
I mean, we could go on and on.

01:44:39.150 --> 01:44:40.770
Let's keep this short and sweet

01:44:40.770 --> 01:44:42.030
because we're going to get
back to the other stuff.

01:44:42.030 --> 01:44:45.760
But it can cause GI
distress at high doses.

01:44:45.760 --> 01:44:50.760
I personally like to take
around 130 or 135 milligrams.

01:44:51.020 --> 01:44:53.880
That way, it's not like
a huge bolus to my gut.

01:44:53.880 --> 01:44:55.860
- But I think it depends on
the form of magnesium too.

01:44:55.860 --> 01:44:57.110
- Yes, yeah.

01:44:57.110 --> 01:45:00.095
I mean, you can take magnesium
threonate, for example,

01:45:00.095 --> 01:45:04.590
and it doesn't affect the gut as much.

01:45:04.590 --> 01:45:05.970
- Magnesium citrate.

01:45:05.970 --> 01:45:07.220
- Citrate is what I take.

01:45:08.820 --> 01:45:10.210
- [Rhonda] I take Thorne.
- It's a pretty potent

01:45:10.210 --> 01:45:12.042
gut stimulus.

01:45:12.042 --> 01:45:14.440
I feel like it's a little
bit harder to digest.

01:45:14.440 --> 01:45:17.050
- 135 milligrams should be pretty good.

01:45:17.050 --> 01:45:18.770
And citrate, actually,

01:45:18.770 --> 01:45:20.290
oh boy, do we want to go here?

01:45:20.290 --> 01:45:24.110
- I mean, it's up to
you. We don't have to.

01:45:24.110 --> 01:45:25.880
Personally, I've been
supplementing with magnesium

01:45:25.880 --> 01:45:26.793
for a long time.

01:45:28.260 --> 01:45:29.960
I use threonate and bisglycinate

01:45:32.898 --> 01:45:35.260
and malate for different reasons.

01:45:35.260 --> 01:45:37.810
So, yes, I would love to
go there if you're willing.

01:45:37.810 --> 01:45:39.747
- I would say malate would be the best,

01:45:39.747 --> 01:45:42.430
and that has to do with
the short-chain fatty acids

01:45:42.430 --> 01:45:44.210
being good for the gut
and a lot of work done

01:45:44.210 --> 01:45:45.840
by a former colleague
of mine and good friend,

01:45:45.840 --> 01:45:48.380
Mark Shigenaga, showing that
the short-chain fatty acid,

01:45:48.380 --> 01:45:50.300
citrate, malate, lactate,

01:45:50.300 --> 01:45:52.763
but specifically malate,
really, and lactate

01:45:52.763 --> 01:45:56.810
are the major ones that get
into the gut epithelial cells

01:45:56.810 --> 01:45:58.610
and are an energy source
for the mitochondria

01:45:58.610 --> 01:46:02.340
and the goblet cells, so anyways,

01:46:02.340 --> 01:46:04.310
whole other topic.
- That's okay.

01:46:04.310 --> 01:46:08.700
I take malate because I was
told that it would be helpful.

01:46:08.700 --> 01:46:10.240
First of all, it doesn't make me sleepy

01:46:10.240 --> 01:46:12.220
like some of the other forms of magnesium,

01:46:12.220 --> 01:46:14.993
which act as a mild sedative for me.

01:46:15.850 --> 01:46:18.310
They do tap into the GABAergic pathway,

01:46:18.310 --> 01:46:20.010
a neurotransmitter, folks,

01:46:20.010 --> 01:46:23.090
that, in general, broad
sweeping generalization here,

01:46:23.090 --> 01:46:26.230
can have somewhat of a sedative quality,

01:46:26.230 --> 01:46:28.910
which is why I take magnesium threonate

01:46:28.910 --> 01:46:30.850
and/or bisglycinate before sleep,

01:46:30.850 --> 01:46:32.650
30 to 60 minutes before sleep.

01:46:32.650 --> 01:46:35.650
Definitely enhances my
transition time to sleep

01:46:36.570 --> 01:46:37.430
and the depth of sleep.

01:46:37.430 --> 01:46:40.100
No question, in my experience.

01:46:40.100 --> 01:46:44.120
There's some data that threonate
can be neuroprotective,

01:46:44.120 --> 01:46:46.820
although those studies are still ongoing.

01:46:46.820 --> 01:46:48.530
I'm getting the sense that maybe you're

01:46:48.530 --> 01:46:50.440
a little more skeptical of that than I am.

01:46:50.440 --> 01:46:53.740
- Yeah, no, I've seen the
studies with the threonate.

01:46:53.740 --> 01:46:56.100
I think, looking at the actual data

01:46:56.100 --> 01:46:59.210
from the one clinical study,

01:46:59.210 --> 01:47:01.140
there wasn't statistical significance

01:47:01.140 --> 01:47:04.810
until all three of the pieces
of data were pulled together,

01:47:04.810 --> 01:47:06.630
but that really could just
be because their sample size

01:47:06.630 --> 01:47:07.920
was too small, right?

01:47:07.920 --> 01:47:09.910
- Yeah, I'm thinking that paired with the,

01:47:09.910 --> 01:47:10.743
there's some work-
- [Rhonda] The animal stuff.

01:47:10.743 --> 01:47:13.063
- Yeah, Guosong Liu's work on.

01:47:14.510 --> 01:47:17.630
This is getting kind of
inside ball of neuroscience.

01:47:17.630 --> 01:47:20.100
The quality of the labs matters, folks,

01:47:20.100 --> 01:47:21.610
and that's something that's not accessible

01:47:21.610 --> 01:47:23.578
to people outside of fields.

01:47:23.578 --> 01:47:27.010
Guosong Liu and some of the
other folks at that time at MIT,

01:47:27.010 --> 01:47:29.420
I think very highly of their work.

01:47:29.420 --> 01:47:32.550
And so the animal studies are
indeed just animal studies,

01:47:32.550 --> 01:47:33.680
but I was pretty impressed

01:47:33.680 --> 01:47:35.240
by what they did in those studies.

01:47:35.240 --> 01:47:38.012
Very pioneering when you
think about this being done

01:47:38.012 --> 01:47:40.290
10, 12, 15 years ago.

01:47:40.290 --> 01:47:42.840
And then, yes, we need
more human clinical data.

01:47:42.840 --> 01:47:46.330
But, for me, I figured that,
given the safety profile

01:47:46.330 --> 01:47:48.620
of mag threonate, given that
it helps me sleep better,

01:47:48.620 --> 01:47:52.830
and sleeping better is just
better for everything, frankly.

01:47:52.830 --> 01:47:53.663
That's why I take it.

01:47:53.663 --> 01:47:54.960
And bisglycinate and threonate

01:47:54.960 --> 01:47:57.020
seem to be somewhat interchangeable,

01:47:57.020 --> 01:47:58.930
but I don't know of any reports

01:47:58.930 --> 01:48:01.010
that bisglycinate can be neuroprotective.

01:48:01.010 --> 01:48:03.113
But malate I take during the daytime.

01:48:04.340 --> 01:48:06.030
For me, and, again, this is subjective,

01:48:06.030 --> 01:48:07.610
it has a tangible effect

01:48:07.610 --> 01:48:10.303
in improving the recovery
time from exercise.

01:48:11.190 --> 01:48:13.350
I don't know that I've
been sore from a workout

01:48:13.350 --> 01:48:14.840
since I started taking malate,

01:48:14.840 --> 01:48:17.000
and I used to get very sore

01:48:17.000 --> 01:48:19.370
from even kind of trivial workouts.

01:48:19.370 --> 01:48:22.400
So I don't know what's going
on there, but I keep taking it.

01:48:22.400 --> 01:48:24.910
- Malate, again, the
short-chain fatty acid,

01:48:24.910 --> 01:48:28.890
and when you do intense exercise,

01:48:28.890 --> 01:48:31.341
you release endotoxin from your gut.

01:48:31.341 --> 01:48:33.603
I'm just going back to
the interesting work

01:48:33.603 --> 01:48:35.930
'cause the malate being
the short-chain fatty acid

01:48:35.930 --> 01:48:38.810
and Mark Shigenaga showing,

01:48:38.810 --> 01:48:41.347
this is all an animal research by the way,

01:48:41.347 --> 01:48:44.150
but it was feeding these animals malate.

01:48:44.150 --> 01:48:46.510
It really protected the
gut, endotoxin release,

01:48:46.510 --> 01:48:49.603
and it affected metabolic
syndrome and all sorts of things.

01:48:49.603 --> 01:48:50.497
But I think malates awesome,

01:48:50.497 --> 01:48:52.650
and I always try to eat green apples.

01:48:52.650 --> 01:48:54.010
They're really high in malic acid.

01:48:54.010 --> 01:48:56.300
- [Andrew] Oh, good to know.
- And tart cherries.

01:48:56.300 --> 01:48:57.910
Tart cherries are really
high in it as well.

01:48:57.910 --> 01:48:59.027
- They also taste really good.

01:48:59.027 --> 01:48:59.957
- But I was really interested

01:48:59.957 --> 01:49:01.700
in the magnesium threonate stuff.

01:49:01.700 --> 01:49:05.730
I take a supplement called
Magnesi-Om by Moon Juice.

01:49:05.730 --> 01:49:07.760
And it's like a little powder.

01:49:07.760 --> 01:49:09.940
It's got a little bit of monk
fruit, but it tastes good.

01:49:09.940 --> 01:49:13.220
So I do it a little bit
before bedtime as well.

01:49:13.220 --> 01:49:14.840
Probably several more hours though

01:49:14.840 --> 01:49:16.810
