01:59:50.690 --> 01:59:51.960
That's been shown.

01:59:51.960 --> 01:59:54.190
- So maybe there's a good opportunity to,

01:59:54.190 --> 01:59:57.380
so cold and UCP1, if you
could educate us on UCP1.

01:59:57.380 --> 01:59:58.213
I find this really interesting,

01:59:58.213 --> 01:59:59.820
and I learned about it from you.

01:59:59.820 --> 02:00:02.210
- Yeah, well, so norepinephrine

02:00:02.210 --> 02:00:03.530
actually released in the plasma.

02:00:03.530 --> 02:00:05.607
It does act as a hormone.

02:00:05.607 --> 02:00:06.960
Vasoconstriction is one thing it does,

02:00:06.960 --> 02:00:10.960
but it also regulates a
variety of molecular functions

02:00:10.960 --> 02:00:13.870
that have to do with adaption to cold,

02:00:13.870 --> 02:00:15.363
one happening to be,

02:00:16.540 --> 02:00:19.730
shivering is a very inefficient
way to produce heat,

02:00:19.730 --> 02:00:20.790
which is what your body's trying to do

02:00:20.790 --> 02:00:22.200
when it's exposed to cold,

02:00:22.200 --> 02:00:24.340
and your muscles are basically contracting

02:00:24.340 --> 02:00:26.960
and producing heat from that,

02:00:26.960 --> 02:00:28.380
but that's just not very efficient.

02:00:28.380 --> 02:00:31.210
So the more eloquent way to do it,

02:00:31.210 --> 02:00:33.120
or elegant, I guess, way to do it

02:00:33.120 --> 02:00:36.700
is to basically have your mitochondria

02:00:36.700 --> 02:00:38.670
produce tons and tons of heat.

02:00:38.670 --> 02:00:41.830
So the way it does this
is by activating a gene

02:00:41.830 --> 02:00:44.830
called UCP1, uncoupling protein 1.

02:00:44.830 --> 02:00:47.330
Norepinephrine is upstream
of that, activating it.

02:00:48.298 --> 02:00:49.750
What that does is, essentially,

02:00:49.750 --> 02:00:51.880
so mitochondria are
these little organelles

02:00:51.880 --> 02:00:53.170
inside of your cells

02:00:53.170 --> 02:00:55.350
that are responsible for producing energy.

02:00:55.350 --> 02:00:59.463
Usually, that's in the form of
adenosine triphosphate, ATP,

02:00:59.463 --> 02:01:02.180
and that's what lets everything function

02:01:02.180 --> 02:01:03.070
inside of your body,

02:01:03.070 --> 02:01:04.420
from your neurotransmitter production

02:01:04.420 --> 02:01:06.203
to your heart beating, et cetera.

02:01:07.070 --> 02:01:10.520
However, you can uncouple
your mitochondria.

02:01:10.520 --> 02:01:12.720
Basically, your mitochondria,

02:01:12.720 --> 02:01:15.290
they're like a little
battery, so they have,

02:01:15.290 --> 02:01:16.480
well, they have a double membrane,

02:01:16.480 --> 02:01:17.410
first of all, their structure,

02:01:17.410 --> 02:01:19.864
but they have a negative
charge on the inside,

02:01:19.864 --> 02:01:22.560
and they have a positive
charge on the inner membrane,

02:01:22.560 --> 02:01:26.847
so in between the outer
membrane and the inside part.

02:01:26.847 --> 02:01:27.680
- Like a neuron.

02:01:27.680 --> 02:01:28.737
- Like a neuron, yeah.

02:01:28.737 --> 02:01:29.570
- [Andrew] Yeah, cool.
- So I guess,

02:01:29.570 --> 02:01:30.403
it's like a neuron.

02:01:30.403 --> 02:01:31.830
It's like a battery,
negative and positive.

02:01:31.830 --> 02:01:34.060
Well, basically, you can
uncouple that charge,

02:01:34.060 --> 02:01:35.650
and so that positive charge proton

02:01:35.650 --> 02:01:37.047
start leaking out of the mitochondria,

02:01:37.047 --> 02:01:38.990
and your mitochondria freak out.

02:01:38.990 --> 02:01:41.093
So this is called uncoupling it.

02:01:42.730 --> 02:01:45.260
It's maximum respiration, as we call it.

02:01:45.260 --> 02:01:47.360
They try to make as much energy.

02:01:47.360 --> 02:01:49.777
They're like, "I got to
get that proton back,

02:01:49.777 --> 02:01:52.040
"that gradient, electrochemical gradient."

02:01:52.040 --> 02:01:55.120
And so they just go insane.

02:01:55.120 --> 02:01:56.700
In this case, it's uncoupled energy,

02:01:56.700 --> 02:01:59.663
so the energy they're making
is actually heat, not ATP,

02:02:01.530 --> 02:02:05.040
but you're essentially burning
substrate, so who cares?

02:02:05.040 --> 02:02:07.840
You're burning glucose.
You're burning lipids.

02:02:07.840 --> 02:02:10.470
You're basically burning
things and making heat.

02:02:10.470 --> 02:02:12.700
And so that's what uncoupling does,

02:02:12.700 --> 02:02:14.580
and that is a much more efficient way

02:02:14.580 --> 02:02:16.410
of producing heat than shivering.

02:02:16.410 --> 02:02:19.040
So, as you become more adapted,

02:02:19.040 --> 02:02:22.360
maybe the longer duration
that you've stayed in the cold

02:02:22.360 --> 02:02:23.600
or the more times you've done it,

02:02:23.600 --> 02:02:25.440
you'll no longer shiver anymore.

02:02:25.440 --> 02:02:28.040
You will start to then just
do this uncoupling type

02:02:28.040 --> 02:02:30.130
of thermogenesis, as it's called.

02:02:30.130 --> 02:02:33.300
And another type of adaptation that occurs

02:02:33.300 --> 02:02:35.720
is you actually produce more mitochondria

02:02:35.720 --> 02:02:37.450
in your adipose tissue.

02:02:37.450 --> 02:02:39.570
And that actually happens,

02:02:39.570 --> 02:02:42.570
also regulated by
norepinephrine or noradrenaline,

02:02:42.570 --> 02:02:46.560
through a protein called PGC-1alpha.

02:02:46.560 --> 02:02:51.560
And what that protein does
is it makes more mitochondria

02:02:51.630 --> 02:02:53.330
in your adipose cell.

02:02:53.330 --> 02:02:55.800
So, per adipose cell, you're
getting more mitochondria.

02:02:55.800 --> 02:02:59.700
It's a beautiful way to
basically make more heat.

02:02:59.700 --> 02:03:02.020
It's one of those things where it's like,

02:03:02.020 --> 02:03:04.037
your body's going, "Okay,
I'm going to be exposed

02:03:04.037 --> 02:03:05.287
"to this cold next time.

02:03:05.287 --> 02:03:07.507
"How can I make sure I don't die?

02:03:07.507 --> 02:03:09.348
"Oh, I can have more mitochondria,

02:03:09.348 --> 02:03:10.387
"and I'm going to make more heat."

02:03:10.387 --> 02:03:12.320
And so you're making more mitochondria

02:03:12.320 --> 02:03:13.390
in your adipose tissue.

02:03:13.390 --> 02:03:17.050
And this is often referred
to as the browning of fat.

02:03:17.050 --> 02:03:18.450
And the reason for that is because,

02:03:18.450 --> 02:03:21.570
if you look under a
microscope at a lipid droplet,

02:03:21.570 --> 02:03:24.790
basically, a fat cell,

02:03:24.790 --> 02:03:26.650
not a lipid droplet, adipocyte,

02:03:26.650 --> 02:03:29.540
you'll find that it looks darker

02:03:29.540 --> 02:03:31.330
because there's more
mitochondria in there.

02:03:31.330 --> 02:03:33.993
So it's referred to as browning fat.

02:03:35.540 --> 02:03:37.680
I don't want to get into
the whole beige fat, brown.

02:03:37.680 --> 02:03:38.790
You know, there's this whole,

02:03:38.790 --> 02:03:39.810
I'm sure you've had experts

02:03:39.810 --> 02:03:41.060
on that talk all about that.
- No, not yet.

02:03:41.060 --> 02:03:43.760
I always think of white
fat, beige fat, brown fat.

02:03:43.760 --> 02:03:45.240
And beige is kind of,

02:03:45.240 --> 02:03:46.870
white can be converted into beige.

02:03:46.870 --> 02:03:48.080
- Right, and beige can take on

02:03:48.080 --> 02:03:50.190
thermogenic characteristics essentially,

02:03:50.190 --> 02:03:53.967
and so you can activate beige
fat so that it's thermogenic

02:03:53.967 --> 02:03:58.967
in the sense that it's burning
glucose and/or fatty acids

02:03:58.980 --> 02:04:01.326
and producing heat.

02:04:01.326 --> 02:04:05.530
So the more you expose yourself to cold,

02:04:05.530 --> 02:04:08.450
the more you can brown
your fat, so to speak,

02:04:08.450 --> 02:04:12.350
and therefore you can tolerate
the cold for longer periods,

02:04:12.350 --> 02:04:13.970
which people do notice,

02:04:13.970 --> 02:04:17.530
and you can then have
the thermogenic qualities

02:04:17.530 --> 02:04:19.750
of having more brown adipose tissue

02:04:19.750 --> 02:04:21.893
or beige, activated beige, adipose tissue.

02:04:23.480 --> 02:04:25.386
You'll get a lot of
naysayers out there saying,

02:04:25.386 --> 02:04:28.487
"Oh, brown fat doesn't
regulate metabolism at all."

02:04:28.487 --> 02:04:31.170
And the reality is there's
thousands of researchers

02:04:31.170 --> 02:04:35.240
trying to pill up brown
fat and thermogenic.

02:04:35.240 --> 02:04:36.660
They're trying to make it a pill

02:04:36.660 --> 02:04:39.180
because it does affect metabolism.

02:04:39.180 --> 02:04:40.210
It's not the only thing.

02:04:40.210 --> 02:04:42.940
It's certainly, if you're obese
and trying to lose weight,

02:04:42.940 --> 02:04:45.770
you're not going to do that
just by doing cold exposure.

02:04:45.770 --> 02:04:50.020
You need to do dietary and
exercise changes predominantly,

02:04:50.020 --> 02:04:52.490
but it does affect metabolism.

02:04:52.490 --> 02:04:56.640
And this has been shown in human studies,

02:04:56.640 --> 02:04:59.303
so it is an interesting,

02:05:00.730 --> 02:05:03.990
it's another possible mechanism
for affecting metabolism,

02:05:03.990 --> 02:05:05.950
and that's an adipose tissue,

02:05:05.950 --> 02:05:09.560
but you also make more
mitochondria and muscle tissue.

02:05:09.560 --> 02:05:12.340
And this is regulated,
not via norepinephrine,

02:05:12.340 --> 02:05:14.563
but it is still PGC-1alpha, interestingly,

02:05:15.889 --> 02:05:17.557
not that anyone else really cares but me,

02:05:17.557 --> 02:05:19.290
and maybe you do, Andrew.

02:05:19.290 --> 02:05:20.880
- I'm eating this up.

02:05:20.880 --> 02:05:25.850
- So PGC-1alpha responds to norepinephrine

02:05:25.850 --> 02:05:28.130
in adipose tissue to
make more mitochondria,

02:05:28.130 --> 02:05:32.250
but, in muscle tissue, it's
unclear what the regulator is.

02:05:32.250 --> 02:05:33.380
Cold exposure does it.

02:05:33.380 --> 02:05:37.650
So this was shown at least in
a couple of studies I've seen,

02:05:37.650 --> 02:05:40.480
where people that were
exercising, I believe,

02:05:40.480 --> 02:05:43.080
or maybe it may have been men
only that were exercising,

02:05:43.080 --> 02:05:46.335
did some training, and then
did cold water immersion,

02:05:46.335 --> 02:05:49.410
something like 50 degrees
Fahrenheit, 15 minutes,

02:05:49.410 --> 02:05:51.490
and PGC-1alpha, which is a biomarker

02:05:51.490 --> 02:05:52.930
for mitochondrial biogenesis,

02:05:52.930 --> 02:05:55.250
which is the generation
of new mitochondria.

02:05:55.250 --> 02:05:56.660
By the way, that's awesome.

02:05:56.660 --> 02:05:58.160
You want more mitochondria in your muscle.

02:05:58.160 --> 02:06:01.070
It's associated with improved muscle mass,

02:06:01.070 --> 02:06:02.580
improved endurance.

02:06:02.580 --> 02:06:04.470
Mitochondria are essentially,

02:06:04.470 --> 02:06:07.030
they're making energy in your cell.

02:06:07.030 --> 02:06:09.450
We don't make more mitochondria normally.

02:06:09.450 --> 02:06:10.720
You have certain inputs,

02:06:10.720 --> 02:06:12.897
high-intensity interval
training, exercise can do it.

02:06:12.897 --> 02:06:14.810
- Can actually make more mitochondria.

02:06:14.810 --> 02:06:17.570
- Yes, yeah, and that's
been shown in people.

02:06:17.570 --> 02:06:20.500
- Weight training or just
high-intensity interval training?

02:06:20.500 --> 02:06:23.300
- I haven't seen weight training.

02:06:23.300 --> 02:06:25.810
I've seen it in high-intensity
interval training,

02:06:25.810 --> 02:06:27.360
endurance training,

02:06:27.360 --> 02:06:29.420
but that doesn't mean
that it hasn't been shown,

02:06:29.420 --> 02:06:31.210
I just haven't seen it, or
that it hasn't been looked at.

02:06:31.210 --> 02:06:32.173
- That's good to know.

02:06:32.173 --> 02:06:36.360
I'm always looking for reasons
