Dynorphin is responsible
for that dysphoric feeling

02:15:34.750 --> 02:15:35.987
when you're in the sauna and you're hot,

02:15:35.987 --> 02:15:37.970
and when you're running, doing exercise,

02:15:37.970 --> 02:15:40.543
and you feel uncomfortable.

02:15:41.770 --> 02:15:43.350
Well, I think that's dynorphin.

02:15:43.350 --> 02:15:44.183
I'm speaking absolute,

02:15:44.183 --> 02:15:45.016
but I think it is.
- No, I think it is.

02:15:45.016 --> 02:15:48.260
I mean, there's evidence in alcoholics

02:15:48.260 --> 02:15:51.150
that some of the symptoms of
withdrawal that they experience

02:15:51.150 --> 02:15:52.453
are related to dynorphin.

02:15:52.453 --> 02:15:56.330
And dynorphin is known
to negatively impact

02:15:56.330 --> 02:15:58.570
the dopamine receptor system.

02:15:58.570 --> 02:16:01.110
So, basically, it's the
feel-like-garbage pathway.

02:16:01.110 --> 02:16:02.140
- Right, you feel like garbage,

02:16:02.140 --> 02:16:05.750
and so you think that
that would not be good,

02:16:05.750 --> 02:16:08.490
but this is where my friend comes in.

02:16:08.490 --> 02:16:09.950
He was looking at the effects

02:16:09.950 --> 02:16:13.600
of treating morphine or heroin addiction.

02:16:13.600 --> 02:16:16.100
And people that are using those drugs,

02:16:16.100 --> 02:16:21.100
they, basically, the endorphins
or the morphine or heroin,

02:16:22.720 --> 02:16:24.290
they bind to a receptor in the brain

02:16:24.290 --> 02:16:26.100
called the mu opioid receptor.

02:16:26.100 --> 02:16:28.700
And, as they take these drugs,

02:16:28.700 --> 02:16:31.640
that mu opioid receptor
becomes downregulated,

02:16:31.640 --> 02:16:34.480
and so you need more and more of the drug

02:16:34.480 --> 02:16:35.840
to feel as good as you did.

02:16:35.840 --> 02:16:38.410
Well, endorphins also
bind to that receptor.

02:16:38.410 --> 02:16:42.000
And he was looking into
some of the other drugs

02:16:42.000 --> 02:16:45.590
that are like salvinoria,
salvinorin, or something.

02:16:45.590 --> 02:16:49.310
Salvia it's called. It binds
to the kappa opioid receptor.

02:16:49.310 --> 02:16:50.800
It also makes you kind
of feel uncomfortable.

02:16:50.800 --> 02:16:54.620
Anyways, he had put some
studies in front of me

02:16:54.620 --> 02:16:59.620
that showed basically
binding of either dynorphin

02:16:59.620 --> 02:17:02.960
or whatever ligand to
the kappa opioid receptor

02:17:02.960 --> 02:17:06.040
basically sensitizes
the mu opioid receptor

02:17:06.040 --> 02:17:08.780
to the feel-good endorphins
and also changes,

02:17:08.780 --> 02:17:11.640
I think it also upregulates
it or something,

02:17:11.640 --> 02:17:14.010
so, basically, there's a
lasting effect of feeling good.

02:17:14.010 --> 02:17:15.660
So the endorphins that you release later

02:17:15.660 --> 02:17:17.390
from hugging someone,

02:17:17.390 --> 02:17:19.980
or a joke you're laughing at, or whatever,

02:17:19.980 --> 02:17:21.940
you feel it for longer, right?

02:17:21.940 --> 02:17:25.900
And so, anyways, with
respect to the sauna,

02:17:25.900 --> 02:17:27.420
it's a big sort of hypothesis of mine.

02:17:27.420 --> 02:17:30.650
I did kind of publish
that part of my hypothesis

02:17:30.650 --> 02:17:33.610
in a review article, but
I do wish more people

02:17:33.610 --> 02:17:34.640
would kind of look into that.

02:17:34.640 --> 02:17:36.850
That'd be amazing.

02:17:36.850 --> 02:17:38.993
But what I was getting at, I think, was,

02:17:40.350 --> 02:17:44.580
I would use the sauna to memorize things.

02:17:44.580 --> 02:17:46.650
This is way back in the
day, and I still do it.

02:17:46.650 --> 02:17:48.358
And I wanted to talk to you about this

02:17:48.358 --> 02:17:50.830
because you're a neuroscientist,

02:17:50.830 --> 02:17:54.670
that there's something
about being in the sauna.

02:17:54.670 --> 02:17:58.330
And I don't know if it has to
do with the stress response.

02:17:58.330 --> 02:18:01.030
Like, when you have an emotional trigger,

02:18:01.030 --> 02:18:02.630
you remember things better, right?

02:18:02.630 --> 02:18:03.610
- Absolutely.

02:18:03.610 --> 02:18:08.140
There is a clear and known
explanation mechanism for this.

02:18:08.140 --> 02:18:12.240
- So, in the sauna, you
also release norepinephrine,

02:18:12.240 --> 02:18:14.350
just like you do in the cold.
There's a lot of overlap.

02:18:15.710 --> 02:18:20.460
It is a stressor, but I
use it to remember things.

02:18:20.460 --> 02:18:21.600
Like, I'm going through something.

02:18:21.600 --> 02:18:23.410
I want to go through a
presentation, or a talk,

02:18:23.410 --> 02:18:24.860
or a podcast, or whatever.

02:18:24.860 --> 02:18:25.980
And I go in that sauna.

02:18:25.980 --> 02:18:28.350
And you should try it,
if you haven't already.

02:18:28.350 --> 02:18:29.183
I don't know if you have.

02:18:29.183 --> 02:18:30.327
- I have a sauna and Cold Plunge now,

02:18:30.327 --> 02:18:32.190
and I haven't tried preparing.

02:18:32.190 --> 02:18:35.330
I read books in the sauna in the evening.

02:18:35.330 --> 02:18:37.980
It's a time I insist on
having my phone out of there,

02:18:39.380 --> 02:18:41.410
initially because I
thought I'd cook the phone,

02:18:41.410 --> 02:18:42.870
but also just to get some separation

02:18:42.870 --> 02:18:45.150
from the phone and screens in
the evening, so I read books.

02:18:45.150 --> 02:18:45.983
The only challenge,

02:18:45.983 --> 02:18:47.230
sometimes you're dripping
sweat onto the books,

02:18:47.230 --> 02:18:49.630
but I'm willing to forego
a few pages of a book.

02:18:52.165 --> 02:18:55.620
The idea that being in this
semi-stressful environment

02:18:55.620 --> 02:18:59.070
would aid in the learning
and retention of information

02:18:59.070 --> 02:19:00.610
is really well substantiated.

02:19:00.610 --> 02:19:02.980
There's this beautiful work
by a guy named James McGaw.

02:19:02.980 --> 02:19:04.250
I don't know if his lab's still active,

02:19:04.250 --> 02:19:06.770
but he was at UC Irvine for a while,

02:19:06.770 --> 02:19:10.410
and then I think at
University of Arizona as well.

02:19:10.410 --> 02:19:12.510
They have a great memory
group at both places,

02:19:12.510 --> 02:19:15.350
very strong in learning
and memory, both places.

02:19:15.350 --> 02:19:17.210
And he was the one that really defined

02:19:17.210 --> 02:19:20.830
this kind of inverted, u-shaped function

02:19:20.830 --> 02:19:24.190
for the relationship between
adrenaline and memory.

02:19:24.190 --> 02:19:27.900
Basically, if you're too
relaxed and not stressed enough,

02:19:27.900 --> 02:19:29.900
you're not going to
remember any information.

02:19:29.900 --> 02:19:34.740
At peak levels of stress, you
actually are a memory machine,

02:19:34.740 --> 02:19:35.770
at least within the context

02:19:35.770 --> 02:19:37.260
of whatever it is you're trying to learn.

02:19:37.260 --> 02:19:40.970
So what you're describing
very well matches with that.

02:19:40.970 --> 02:19:42.480
Then, of course, it tapers off

02:19:42.480 --> 02:19:45.040
as you really increase
adrenaline to the point

02:19:45.040 --> 02:19:47.480
where people are starting
to lose autonomic function,

02:19:47.480 --> 02:19:49.860
where they're panicking basically,

02:19:49.860 --> 02:19:51.250
but obviously, you're keeping it in range.

02:19:51.250 --> 02:19:53.360
The other thing that I would
like to ask you about is,

02:19:53.360 --> 02:19:55.460
in the sauna, of course,
there's vasodilation,

02:19:55.460 --> 02:19:57.670
and perfusion of blood to
the brain is a wonderful way

02:19:57.670 --> 02:19:58.890
to enhance cognition.

02:19:58.890 --> 02:20:00.970
There's even some really
nice data showing that,

02:20:00.970 --> 02:20:03.700
during inhales as opposed to exhales,

02:20:03.700 --> 02:20:06.830
people are better at learning information.

02:20:06.830 --> 02:20:08.390
Believe it or not, during the inhale,

02:20:08.390 --> 02:20:09.800
you're taking in and absorbing

02:20:09.800 --> 02:20:11.510
and remembering more than during exhales.

02:20:11.510 --> 02:20:15.110
And these are beautiful studies
done in humans, of course.

02:20:15.110 --> 02:20:17.780
So I can imagine that vasodilation,

02:20:17.780 --> 02:20:19.620
getting more perfusion
of blood to the brain,

02:20:19.620 --> 02:20:21.160
plus a little bit of stress,

02:20:21.160 --> 02:20:23.493
or maybe a lot of stress
from the epinephrine,

02:20:24.530 --> 02:20:26.740
and then, of course,
there's going to be the,

02:20:26.740 --> 02:20:27.840
I don't want to call it placebo,

02:20:27.840 --> 02:20:29.720
but there's going to be the context,

02:20:29.720 --> 02:20:32.010
the condition, place context of it.

02:20:32.010 --> 02:20:34.260
Like, if we had a good experience

02:20:34.260 --> 02:20:36.350
remembering something in the sauna once,

02:20:36.350 --> 02:20:38.840
the positive association effect
of that location is real.

02:20:38.840 --> 02:20:42.230
Just like, if people go to a
new city and they get robbed,

02:20:42.230 --> 02:20:43.400
like, if you go to Cincinnati,

02:20:43.400 --> 02:20:44.240
I've never been to Cincinnati,

02:20:44.240 --> 02:20:45.430
but you get robbed in Cincinnati,

02:20:45.430 --> 02:20:47.280
your purse gets taken or
your wallet gets taken,

02:20:47.280 --> 02:20:49.470
you kind of hate Cincinnati as a tourist.

02:20:49.470 --> 02:20:50.900
But that could happen in any number

02:20:50.900 --> 02:20:52.010
of different cities, right?

02:20:52.010 --> 02:20:53.430
The opposite is also true,

02:20:53.430 --> 02:20:55.130
so if something good happens someplace.

02:20:55.130 --> 02:20:57.310
So I'm imagining that it's a
combination of those effects,

02:20:57.310 --> 02:21:00.840
but it would be very hard
to do this in the cold.

02:21:00.840 --> 02:21:02.823
I feel like the cold is a very potent.

02:21:02.823 --> 02:21:04.950
I think it takes you
too far down that curve,

02:21:04.950 --> 02:21:05.830
the McGaw curve.

02:21:05.830 --> 02:21:07.812
- I have to sing songs or
something when I'm in there.

02:21:07.812 --> 02:21:09.150
- [Andrew] Distract yourself.
- Oh yeah, I sing songs.

02:21:09.150 --> 02:21:10.880
- But afterward, you're
very efficient at learning.

02:21:10.880 --> 02:21:12.290
- After I am.

02:21:12.290 --> 02:21:15.400
With respect to the sauna,
the vasodilation does occur,

02:21:15.400 --> 02:21:16.770
so there's a lot of overlap

02:21:16.770 --> 02:21:20.670
between moderate-intensity
aerobic exercise and heat stress.

02:21:20.670 --> 02:21:22.370
As you can imagine,
when you're exercising,

02:21:22.370 --> 02:21:25.160
you're elevating your
core body temperature.

02:21:25.160 --> 02:21:26.810
You're sweating.

02:21:26.810 --> 02:21:28.450
When you're actually in the sauna,

02:21:28.450 --> 02:21:31.320
blood does get redistributed to the skin

02:21:31.320 --> 02:21:32.530
to facilitate sweating,

02:21:32.530 --> 02:21:37.170
but, much like exercise, blood
flow in general is improved

02:21:37.170 --> 02:21:39.530
to the brain, to the muscles, everywhere.

02:21:39.530 --> 02:21:42.914
So, I think, generally speaking,

02:21:42.914 --> 02:21:46.320
and there's studies showing
that sauna use is associated

02:21:46.320 --> 02:21:49.660
with a much lower risk of
dementia and Alzheimer's disease.

02:21:49.660 --> 02:21:52.160
People that use it four
to seven times a week

02:21:52.160 --> 02:21:55.620
have greater than 60%
reduction in dementia

02:21:55.620 --> 02:21:56.917
and Alzheimer's disease risk

02:21:56.917 --> 02:21:57.925
compared to once-
- Amazing, how many?

02:21:57.925 --> 02:21:58.980
Oh, sorry, I didn't mean to cut you off.

02:21:58.980 --> 02:22:00.580
You said people who use it,

02:22:00.580 --> 02:22:02.940
I apologize, but maybe
you could tell us again,

02:22:02.940 --> 02:22:05.853
people use it four to seven
times per week have...

02:22:05.853 --> 02:22:08.900
- They have a greater than 60% reduction

02:22:08.900 --> 02:22:11.340
in dementia risk and
Alzheimer's disease risk

02:22:11.340 --> 02:22:14.250
compared to people that use
it only one time a week.

02:22:14.250 --> 02:22:16.260
People that use it two
to three times a week

02:22:16.260 --> 02:22:18.290
have something like a 20,

02:22:18.290 --> 02:22:19.523
a little greater than
20% reduction in risk.

02:22:19.523 --> 02:22:23.330
There's a dose-dependent
effect on dementia risk

02:22:23.330 --> 02:22:25.410
and Alzheimer's disease risk.

02:22:25.410 --> 02:22:27.941
It also has a profound.

02:22:27.941 --> 02:22:29.190
There's a big link

02:22:29.190 --> 02:22:31.790
between the cardiovascular
system and the brain.

02:22:31.790 --> 02:22:34.060
Obviously, blood flow, a big one, right?

02:22:34.060 --> 02:22:35.963
You need to get blood to your brain.

02:22:36.860 --> 02:22:39.670
But cardiovascular mortality,

02:22:39.670 --> 02:22:41.980
so mortality from cardiovascular disease.

02:22:41.980 --> 02:22:43.740
If people use, or actually this was men.

02:22:43.740 --> 02:22:46.370
If men use the sauna four
to seven times a week,

02:22:46.370 --> 02:22:50.100
it's a 50% reduction in
cardiovascular related mortality

02:22:50.100 --> 02:22:51.760
compared to one time a week.

02:22:51.760 --> 02:22:54.220
Again, dose-dependent manner,
two to three times a week

02:22:54.220 --> 02:22:57.610
is something like 24% lower death

02:22:57.610 --> 02:22:58.900
from cardiovascular disease.

02:22:58.900 --> 02:23:02.150
There's also lower sudden cardiac deaths,

02:23:02.150 --> 02:23:03.130
like a heart attack.

02:23:03.130 --> 02:23:06.470
That's like 60 something,
greater than 60% lower,

02:23:06.470 --> 02:23:09.410
if men use it four to seven
times a week versus once.

02:23:09.410 --> 02:23:11.540
Again, a dose-dependent thing.

02:23:11.540 --> 02:23:13.520
The thing that's so
profound there also to me

02:23:13.520 --> 02:23:15.100
when, again, looking at the methods,

02:23:15.100 --> 02:23:16.210
when I look at the data,

02:23:16.210 --> 02:23:19.400
and this is all work
from Dr. Jari Laukkanen.

02:23:19.400 --> 02:23:22.759
He's in the University of Eastern Finland

02:23:22.759 --> 02:23:25.890
and just one of the world
experts on sauna use,

02:23:25.890 --> 02:23:28.030
especially with respect
to cardiovascular health.

02:23:28.030 --> 02:23:31.600
What some of his data
has also shown is that,

02:23:31.600 --> 02:23:34.160
if you look at the duration,
the time spent in the sauna,

02:23:34.160 --> 02:23:36.950
so I mentioned the
temperature I do is about,

02:23:36.950 --> 02:23:39.390
I do 189 degrees Fahrenheit.

02:23:39.390 --> 02:23:41.080
Typically, I go in there.

02:23:41.080 --> 02:23:43.496
I'm pretty heat adapted.

02:23:43.496 --> 02:23:47.640
So the more you do the sauna
or any sort of heat stress,

02:23:47.640 --> 02:23:51.307
whether it's a hot tub or
jacuzzi, you become adapted.

02:23:51.307 --> 02:23:52.670
You basically start to sweat

02:23:52.670 --> 02:23:55.130
at a lower core body temperature
to cool yourself down.

02:23:55.130 --> 02:23:56.530
All these sort of physiological changes

02:23:56.530 --> 02:23:58.550
start to happen earlier.

02:23:58.550 --> 02:24:00.964
And so, I stayed in for like 30 minutes,

02:24:00.964 --> 02:24:02.980
so I stayed in a long time.

02:24:02.980 --> 02:24:04.810
That's a lot. You have
to listen to your body.

02:24:04.810 --> 02:24:07.243
Most of the studies that
I just talked about,

02:24:08.880 --> 02:24:11.310
the duration, the time spent in the sauna,

02:24:11.310 --> 02:24:12.970
when I said 50% reduction

02:24:12.970 --> 02:24:15.590
in cardiovascular disease related death,

02:24:15.590 --> 02:24:17.990
what was shown was that
men that were in the sauna

02:24:17.990 --> 02:24:19.760
for only 11 minutes,

02:24:19.760 --> 02:24:21.570
even if they used it four
to seven times a week,

02:24:21.570 --> 02:24:24.720
that reduction was only
like 8% instead of 50.

02:24:24.720 --> 02:24:27.733
It had to be greater than 19 minutes,

02:24:27.733 --> 02:24:29.810
so like 20 minutes is the sweet spot,

02:24:29.810 --> 02:24:32.873
at about 174 degrees Fahrenheit.

02:24:34.550 --> 02:24:36.692
And most of the saunas
in Finland, by the way,

02:24:36.692 --> 02:24:41.120
they're humid, so they put hot water,

02:24:41.120 --> 02:24:44.030
they put water on hot
rocks to create steam,

02:24:44.030 --> 02:24:46.660
so it's usually between 10 to 20% humidity

02:24:46.660 --> 02:24:48.060
in the Finnish sauna.

02:24:48.060 --> 02:24:50.800
So those studies were, I
would say, most of the time,

02:24:50.800 --> 02:24:53.570
you're going to find that their
humidity is also elevated.

02:24:53.570 --> 02:24:56.920
But, to me, the
dose-dependent nature of it

02:24:56.920 --> 02:24:57.770
and the duration,

02:24:59.220 --> 02:25:03.940
to me, that's very strong
data that this is more causal

02:25:03.940 --> 02:25:05.690
than some corollary thing.

02:25:05.690 --> 02:25:07.290
Because that's always the problem

02:25:07.290 --> 02:25:09.880
with observational
studies, including these,

02:25:09.880 --> 02:25:12.300
which they corrected for
a whole host of factors,

02:25:12.300 --> 02:25:15.270
like cholesterol,
exercise, just everything,

02:25:15.270 --> 02:25:17.310
everything under the sun,
they corrected for those.

02:25:17.310 --> 02:25:18.720
And, on top of that,

02:25:18.720 --> 02:25:20.437
you have the dose-dependent
nature of the duration,

02:25:20.437 --> 02:25:23.490
the time spent in the
sauna, and the frequency.

02:25:23.490 --> 02:25:26.170
So, to me, it's like,
something's going on here.

02:25:26.170 --> 02:25:27.330
- Yeah.
- Plus there's been studies,

02:25:27.330 --> 02:25:28.163
intervention studies,

02:25:28.163 --> 02:25:31.030
where it's comparing
directly, head-to-head

02:25:31.030 --> 02:25:34.490
moderate-intensity aerobic
exercise on a stationary cycle

02:25:34.490 --> 02:25:36.793
to 20 minutes in a sauna.

02:25:37.820 --> 02:25:39.500
Physiologically the same things happen,

02:25:39.500 --> 02:25:44.340
so heart rate elevates while
you're doing the activity,

02:25:44.340 --> 02:25:46.420
blood pressure increases while
you're doing the activity,

02:25:46.420 --> 02:25:49.290
but then, after, heart rate decreases,

02:25:49.290 --> 02:25:51.640
resting heart rate
decreases below baseline,

02:25:51.640 --> 02:25:54.410
blood pressure is improved, so
it decreases below baseline.

02:25:54.410 --> 02:25:55.590
This is happening the same

02:25:55.590 --> 02:25:58.220
in moderate-intensity
cycling versus sauna.

02:25:58.220 --> 02:26:01.030
So, again, the sauna, this heat stress,

02:26:01.030 --> 02:26:03.780
there's something about
it that really mimics

02:26:03.780 --> 02:26:06.390
this moderate-intensity aerobic exercise,

02:26:06.390 --> 02:26:10.240
which is really great for
people that can't go for a run,

02:26:10.240 --> 02:26:13.740
that can't even get on a
bike, so disabled people.

02:26:13.740 --> 02:26:15.640
Granted, there are some safety concerns.

02:26:15.640 --> 02:26:18.780
They're pretty mild, but they do exist.

02:26:18.780 --> 02:26:20.280
So people that had a recent heart attack

02:26:20.280 --> 02:26:23.280
or have some rare kind of
heart disease or problem.

02:26:23.280 --> 02:26:24.450
Drinking alcohol, never do that.

02:26:24.450 --> 02:26:27.330
Elderly people prone
to low blood pressure.

02:26:27.330 --> 02:26:29.440
Always talk to a physician
before doing the sauna.

02:26:29.440 --> 02:26:30.950
It is stressful.

02:26:30.950 --> 02:26:32.610
- Pregnant.
- Pregnant women, yeah.

02:26:32.610 --> 02:26:35.023
I definitely avoided
saunas when I was pregnant.

02:26:37.880 --> 02:26:39.510
I think it's very relevant
for disabled people

02:26:39.510 --> 02:26:41.160
and also people that are sedentary

02:26:41.160 --> 02:26:42.300
or been sedentary most of their life.

02:26:42.300 --> 02:26:45.870
Like, my mother, I've been
able to get her in the sauna

02:26:45.870 --> 02:26:47.210
because she's not.

02:26:47.210 --> 02:26:49.400
I did get her on the Peloton once,

02:26:49.400 --> 02:26:50.760
but it's really much easier.

02:26:50.760 --> 02:26:53.070
She feels like it's a spa treatment,

02:26:53.070 --> 02:26:56.450
and she can listen to her music in there.

02:26:56.450 --> 02:26:57.810
Like, I care about her health,

02:26:57.810 --> 02:27:00.270
but she's mostly been a sedentary person.

02:27:00.270 --> 02:27:03.890
And so I find it much
easier to convince her

02:27:03.890 --> 02:27:06.340
to get in the sauna
than to get on Peloton.

02:27:06.340 --> 02:27:07.240
Ideally, you do both.

02:27:07.240 --> 02:27:10.030
The question would be,
well, I exercise, I run,

02:27:10.030 --> 02:27:12.140
I do my high-intensity interval training.

02:27:12.140 --> 02:27:13.850
Why do I need to get in the sauna?

02:27:13.850 --> 02:27:16.265
And the reality is,

02:27:16.265 --> 02:27:18.380
and so I published all this in a review

02:27:18.380 --> 02:27:20.860
in the Experimental Dermatology

02:27:20.860 --> 02:27:22.933
last year, I guess, late last year.

02:27:24.570 --> 02:27:27.370
Basically, cardiorespiratory fitness,

02:27:27.370 --> 02:27:29.350
which is a marker of,

02:27:29.350 --> 02:27:30.913
it's a marker of health.

02:27:31.950 --> 02:27:34.440
Cardiorespiratory fitness is improved

02:27:34.440 --> 02:27:37.370
in people that do exercise and sauna

02:27:37.370 --> 02:27:40.020
compared to exercise alone or sauna alone.

02:27:40.020 --> 02:27:42.960
So, for those healthy,
fit people out there

02:27:42.960 --> 02:27:44.550
already exercising,

02:27:44.550 --> 02:27:47.320
there's a synergistic effect

02:27:47.320 --> 02:27:49.830
by also adding a sauna into that routine.

02:27:49.830 --> 02:27:51.660
And, to me, that's great.

02:27:51.660 --> 02:27:54.630
And there's so many beneficial things

02:27:54.630 --> 02:27:56.750
happening with the heat stress.

02:27:56.750 --> 02:27:59.070
In addition to mimicking aerobic exercise,

02:27:59.070 --> 02:28:00.210
there's the heat shock proteins

02:28:00.210 --> 02:28:01.630
that we talked about earlier.

02:28:01.630 --> 02:28:03.400
And those, it kind of brings me back

02:28:03.400 --> 02:28:05.590
to my early days of science

02:28:05.590 --> 02:28:08.650
when I at the Salk Institute
for Biological Studies

02:28:08.650 --> 02:28:12.150
doing research on little
nematode worms that we

02:28:14.060 --> 02:28:17.800
or someone else injected amyloid beta 42,

02:28:17.800 --> 02:28:22.150
the peptide, the 42-amino
acid peptide that is involved

02:28:22.150 --> 02:28:24.830
in amyloid plaques found in the brain

02:28:24.830 --> 02:28:26.220
correlating with Alzheimer's disease

02:28:26.220 --> 02:28:28.160
and other brain disorders.

02:28:28.160 --> 02:28:31.490
We injected those into the
muscle tissue of worms.

02:28:31.490 --> 02:28:34.700
And, basically, these worms
become paralyzed with age

02:28:34.700 --> 02:28:37.153
because the aggregated proteins,

02:28:38.370 --> 02:28:40.200
these proteins aggregate.

02:28:40.200 --> 02:28:41.360
Well, heat shock proteins,

02:28:41.360 --> 02:28:44.480
one of the main things they
do is they basically make sure

02:28:44.480 --> 02:28:46.450
the proteins inside of your cells

02:28:46.450 --> 02:28:48.980
maintain their proper
three-dimensional structure

02:28:48.980 --> 02:28:51.960
and are folded right, and so they don't,

02:28:51.960 --> 02:28:55.890
they're not prone to aggregating
and forming these plaques

02:28:55.890 --> 02:28:57.873
in your arteries and also in the brain.

02:28:59.300 --> 02:29:02.390
Back to my worm studies I was doing,

02:29:02.390 --> 02:29:04.517
I would elevate heat shock
proteins in these worms,

02:29:04.517 --> 02:29:07.410
and it would totally correct the problem,

02:29:07.410 --> 02:29:09.770
where they would no
longer become paralyzed.

02:29:09.770 --> 02:29:11.120
They'd move around like they were young.

02:29:11.120 --> 02:29:13.380
So, many animal studies have been done

02:29:13.380 --> 02:29:15.493
looking at Alzheimer's disease,

02:29:17.817 --> 02:29:20.270
a human-like Alzheimer's
disease in a rodent,

02:29:20.270 --> 02:29:22.420
and heat shock proteins
protecting from it.

02:29:23.310 --> 02:29:26.440
Heat shock proteins are
robustly activated in humans.

02:29:26.440 --> 02:29:28.363
This has been shown to,

02:29:29.280 --> 02:29:33.650
even 50% higher over baseline
levels after just 30 minutes

02:29:33.650 --> 02:29:36.253
at 163 degrees Fahrenheit in the sauna.

02:29:37.640 --> 02:29:39.740
And they stay activated,
at least, in rodents,

02:29:39.740 --> 02:29:42.433
for 48 hours, at least.

02:29:43.283 --> 02:29:46.960
So having these heat
shock proteins around,

02:29:46.960 --> 02:29:50.050
making sure they're properly
taking care of our proteins

02:29:50.050 --> 02:29:52.830
so they're not aggregating in
our brains and in our plaques,

02:29:52.830 --> 02:29:55.830
could be another potential
way that sauna's protecting

02:29:55.830 --> 02:30:00.380
from Alzheimer's disease and
other cardiovascular health

02:30:00.380 --> 02:30:01.823
as well as longevity.

02:30:03.530 --> 02:30:05.230
There's people that have SNPs

02:30:05.230 --> 02:30:08.150
in heat shock protein factor 70

02:30:08.150 --> 02:30:10.300
that, if they have one of them,

02:30:10.300 --> 02:30:11.450
so they got one from their parents

02:30:11.450 --> 02:30:13.640
where they have more active
heat shock protein 70,

02:30:13.640 --> 02:30:15.350
they live on average one year longer

02:30:15.350 --> 02:30:16.870
than people that don't have that SNP.

02:30:16.870 --> 02:30:18.200
And if they have two versions,

02:30:18.200 --> 02:30:20.380
if they got one from their
mom and one from their dad,

02:30:20.380 --> 02:30:21.893
they live on average two years longer

02:30:21.893 --> 02:30:23.085
than people that don't have that SNP.

02:30:23.085 --> 02:30:24.620
- [Andrew] Wow.
- So it's also been associated

02:30:24.620 --> 02:30:26.770
with human longevity as
well as in lower organisms.

02:30:26.770 --> 02:30:28.780
So you can heat shock a worm or a fly

02:30:28.780 --> 02:30:30.850
and they live 15% longer.

02:30:30.850 --> 02:30:33.207
This is work done by Gordon
Lithgow at the Buck Institute

02:30:33.207 --> 02:30:34.723
years and years ago.

02:30:36.245 --> 02:30:37.760
So, anyways,

02:30:37.760 --> 02:30:40.940
I guess what I was getting at
was the heat shock proteins

02:30:40.940 --> 02:30:42.470
are part of that stress response pathway

02:30:42.470 --> 02:30:43.920
that we talked about earlier.

02:30:45.060 --> 02:30:46.840
They're also activated by cold as well.

02:30:46.840 --> 02:30:49.050
Cold shock does activate
heat shock proteins.

02:30:49.050 --> 02:30:51.510
Not as robust. Sulforaphane
activates them.

02:30:51.510 --> 02:30:52.343
Again, it's one of the reasons

02:30:52.343 --> 02:30:54.260
I think we should get all of these things

02:30:54.260 --> 02:30:57.500
because they are more robust inputs.

02:30:57.500 --> 02:31:00.530
The input activating
mechanisms are more robust

02:31:00.530 --> 02:31:02.040
for different ones.

02:31:02.040 --> 02:31:03.433
So there is crosstalk.

02:31:04.810 --> 02:31:08.810
I guess it'd be more accurate
to say there's overlap.

02:31:08.810 --> 02:31:11.820
But it's also like,

02:31:11.820 --> 02:31:14.010
you want to get the most
robust from all of them, right?

02:31:14.010 --> 02:31:18.070
I do, so that's why I want
to do the sauna and exercise

02:31:18.070 --> 02:31:23.070
and eat my broccoli
sprouts and all that stuff.

02:31:23.430 --> 02:31:25.560
- It's super interesting.

02:31:25.560 --> 02:31:28.230
A couple of questions came up for me.

02:31:28.230 --> 02:31:31.320
One is, you mentioned these SNPs,

02:31:31.320 --> 02:31:33.190
these nucleotide repeats,

02:31:33.190 --> 02:31:35.160
basically genes that
some people have more of

02:31:35.160 --> 02:31:37.020
or less of than others

02:31:37.020 --> 02:31:40.130
that can predict longevity in some sense.

02:31:40.130 --> 02:31:42.600
Is that the FOXO3 pathway?

02:31:42.600 --> 02:31:43.900
- That's one that can.

02:31:43.900 --> 02:31:46.870
Yeah, I mean, FOXO3 is, in fact,

02:31:46.870 --> 02:31:48.890
if you go back to the worm
studies I was talking about,

02:31:48.890 --> 02:31:50.500
that was one of the first things

02:31:50.500 --> 02:31:51.870
when you see it with your own eyes.

02:31:51.870 --> 02:31:54.400
You can take these worms that,

02:31:54.400 --> 02:31:57.730
you basically decrease their
insulin signaling pathway

02:31:57.730 --> 02:31:59.080
and their IGF-1.

02:31:59.080 --> 02:32:01.540
Worms have what are
called homologous genes,

02:32:01.540 --> 02:32:04.220
so they have a lot of
similarities to humans.

02:32:04.220 --> 02:32:05.467
They have an insulin-like receptor,

02:32:05.467 --> 02:32:07.810
they have an IGF-1-like receptor,

02:32:07.810 --> 02:32:10.490
and they make something
like FOXO3, which we have.

02:32:10.490 --> 02:32:13.360
And, basically, if you decrease

02:32:13.360 --> 02:32:14.620
that insulin signaling pathway,

02:32:14.620 --> 02:32:17.400
then FOXO3 is always
active in those worms,

02:32:17.400 --> 02:32:19.320
and they live like 100% longer.

02:32:19.320 --> 02:32:21.850
And not only do they live longer,

02:32:21.850 --> 02:32:25.110
they are like a very young worm.

02:32:25.110 --> 02:32:27.547
I mean, you look at this
thing and you're like,

02:32:27.547 --> 02:32:30.867
"This looks like the worm that
was just born like hours ago.

02:32:30.867 --> 02:32:33.520
"What's going on? This thing's
at the end of its life."

02:32:33.520 --> 02:32:35.077
Now, as a side note,

02:32:35.077 --> 02:32:37.770
the thing that always got me on this was,

02:32:37.770 --> 02:32:40.640
by the way, this was
discovered by Cynthia Kenyon.

02:32:40.640 --> 02:32:42.293
This was back in the '90s.

02:32:43.180 --> 02:32:44.297
Honestly, I'm not sure that anything

02:32:44.297 --> 02:32:46.550
has been as exciting in
the worm world since then,

02:32:46.550 --> 02:32:49.093
but I thought it was a really big finding.

02:32:50.231 --> 02:32:51.910
The only caveat there

02:32:51.910 --> 02:32:53.940
is that the worms go through this dauer.

02:32:53.940 --> 02:32:56.720
It's called a dauer
stage when this happens,

02:32:56.720 --> 02:32:58.920
when you decrease their
insulin signaling and stuff,

02:32:58.920 --> 02:33:01.430
and they go into this
like metabolic stasis.

02:33:01.430 --> 02:33:03.140
They're not eating as much or moving.

02:33:03.140 --> 02:33:05.520
And so it's like, okay,
well they live 100% longer,

02:33:05.520 --> 02:33:08.560
but they go into this
weird state, you know?

02:33:08.560 --> 02:33:09.490
- I know people like this,

02:33:09.490 --> 02:33:11.450
some in the longevity community.

02:33:11.450 --> 02:33:13.260
They know who they are.

02:33:13.260 --> 02:33:15.580
But they'll get the last
laugh 'cause I'll be dead,

02:33:15.580 --> 02:33:18.823
well fed but dead, and
they'll still be going.

02:33:19.860 --> 02:33:23.640
So, in terms of the many data on sauna,

02:33:23.640 --> 02:33:27.430
and I also just want to
acknowledge these Finnish groups

02:33:27.430 --> 02:33:28.263
that did this work.

02:33:28.263 --> 02:33:29.310
It's really pioneering, right?

02:33:29.310 --> 02:33:30.570
When you think 20 years ago,

02:33:30.570 --> 02:33:32.760
long before social media or any of this,

02:33:32.760 --> 02:33:34.070
and they're out there,

02:33:34.070 --> 02:33:36.110
up there, I should say,

02:33:36.110 --> 02:33:39.850
measuring cortisol and growth
hormone and all this stuff

02:33:39.850 --> 02:33:41.910
in people getting in and out of sauna.

02:33:41.910 --> 02:33:43.173
Very, very interesting.

02:33:44.020 --> 02:33:47.030
So 20 minutes seems like the threshold

02:33:47.030 --> 02:33:49.423
at 170 degrees Fahrenheit.

02:33:51.220 --> 02:33:54.280
More times per week seems
to be better than fewer

02:33:54.280 --> 02:33:56.990
in terms of all-cause
mortality, cardiovascular risk

02:33:56.990 --> 02:33:58.570
according to what I just learned from you.

02:33:58.570 --> 02:34:01.430
- Four would be a good, I
think, minimum effective dose.

02:34:01.430 --> 02:34:04.100
- Four times a week, and you
combine it with the cold.

02:34:04.100 --> 02:34:06.423
I've also seen a protocol, where,

02:34:06.423 --> 02:34:08.130
it's a very extreme protocol,

02:34:08.130 --> 02:34:09.910
I don't recommend this to
people right off the bat,

02:34:09.910 --> 02:34:11.950
where they had human subjects

02:34:11.950 --> 02:34:14.390
get into the sauna for 30
minutes, get out for five,

02:34:14.390 --> 02:34:16.180
30 minutes, get out for five, 30 minutes,

02:34:16.180 --> 02:34:18.550
for a total of two hours of exposure,

02:34:18.550 --> 02:34:22.750
but that was what led to these
massive 16-fold increases

02:34:22.750 --> 02:34:23.713
in growth hormone.

02:34:25.790 --> 02:34:27.220
And they had to do it very seldom.

02:34:27.220 --> 02:34:28.900
So it sounds like these
protocols you're describing,

02:34:28.900 --> 02:34:31.077
20 minutes done four times per week,

02:34:31.077 --> 02:34:33.860
are far more reasonable
for most people to access.

02:34:33.860 --> 02:34:37.330
But I know people are
probably desperate to know,

02:34:37.330 --> 02:34:39.070
what if they don't have a sauna?

02:34:39.070 --> 02:34:40.720
A sauna is kind of a unique item,

02:34:40.720 --> 02:34:42.030
so I have a couple questions.

02:34:42.030 --> 02:34:43.463
Can people use hot baths?

02:34:44.470 --> 02:34:46.900
With the appropriate warning, of course.

02:34:46.900 --> 02:34:48.190
Without getting into description

02:34:48.190 --> 02:34:50.240
of the mechanics and
the underlying biology,

02:34:50.240 --> 02:34:54.400
it's pretty obvious that the testes,

02:34:54.400 --> 02:34:57.530
if they get too warm, you'll kill sperm.

02:34:57.530 --> 02:34:59.610
That's the reason why
the testes are housed

02:34:59.610 --> 02:35:02.240
in a structure called the
scrotum that can move around,

02:35:02.240 --> 02:35:03.490
so just to be, you know,

02:35:03.490 --> 02:35:06.830
we are biologists, just
talking about realities here.

02:35:06.830 --> 02:35:08.950
So, if you're trying to conceive children

02:35:08.950 --> 02:35:10.870
or keep your sperm healthy,

02:35:10.870 --> 02:35:13.330
guys should probably stay
out of warm, hot baths.

02:35:13.330 --> 02:35:14.210
- For at least six months.

02:35:14.210 --> 02:35:16.739
That's been shown.
- [Andrew] Six months.

02:35:16.739 --> 02:35:18.440
- So sperm motility goes down

02:35:18.440 --> 02:35:20.650
and sperm production goes down,

02:35:20.650 --> 02:35:23.450
but that is completely corrected

02:35:23.450 --> 02:35:25.240
if they stay out of the
sauna for six months.

02:35:25.240 --> 02:35:27.670
So through six months
later, it's back to normal.

02:35:27.670 --> 02:35:29.970
- Great, that's a very
useful information, I'm sure,

02:35:29.970 --> 02:35:32.290
to a number of people out there.

02:35:32.290 --> 02:35:36.987
So, if people don't
have access to a sauna,

02:35:36.987 --> 02:35:37.957
and we get this about cold too.

02:35:37.957 --> 02:35:40.040
People always say, "What
about cold showers?"

02:35:40.040 --> 02:35:40.873
And I always say, well,

02:35:40.873 --> 02:35:42.850
the studies have mainly
been done on immersion

02:35:42.850 --> 02:35:45.590
'cause it's hard to keep things
controlled in cold showers.

02:35:45.590 --> 02:35:47.010
It just doesn't make for
a very good experiment

02:35:47.010 --> 02:35:49.150
'cause you get a bigger person,

02:35:49.150 --> 02:35:50.520
less of them is under the shower,

02:35:50.520 --> 02:35:51.940
and so it doesn't make
for a good experiment.

02:35:51.940 --> 02:35:53.650
So it's not as good as immersion,

02:35:53.650 --> 02:35:56.190
but, with heat, I could
imagine that a hot bath

02:35:56.190 --> 02:35:58.000
would work almost as well.

02:35:58.000 --> 02:35:59.810
- Yeah, so there's been
some studies looking at,

02:35:59.810 --> 02:36:01.700
for example, activation
of heat shock proteins,

02:36:01.700 --> 02:36:03.890
also brain-derived
neurotrophic factor increases

02:36:03.890 --> 02:36:04.990
with heat stress.

02:36:04.990 --> 02:36:09.550
And so the hot bath at around
104 degrees Fahrenheit,

02:36:09.550 --> 02:36:12.320
which is typically what studies
will use for temperature,

02:36:12.320 --> 02:36:14.140
which is actually cooler than what I.

02:36:14.140 --> 02:36:15.690
I crank my bath hot. It's so hot.

02:36:15.690 --> 02:36:17.740
- But you're very heat adapted.

02:36:17.740 --> 02:36:19.090
- I'm very heat adapted, yeah.

02:36:19.090 --> 02:36:21.570
And it's 20 minutes
from the shoulders down,

02:36:21.570 --> 02:36:25.180
and that is a very robust
activation in heat shock proteins

02:36:25.180 --> 02:36:28.040
and in brain-derived neurotrophic factor.

02:36:28.040 --> 02:36:30.440
And then, heat shock
proteins are also protecting

02:36:30.440 --> 02:36:31.530
against muscle atrophy,

02:36:31.530 --> 02:36:33.570
so that's also having to do
with the protein structure

02:36:33.570 --> 02:36:34.720
and the muscle tissue as well.

02:36:34.720 --> 02:36:38.270
And this has been studies in animal data

02:36:38.270 --> 02:36:40.480
as well as some recent human data as well.

02:36:40.480 --> 02:36:43.150
It was local hyperthermia
or local heat treatment,

02:36:43.150 --> 02:36:45.633
but essentially it
showed that it protected.

02:36:46.840 --> 02:36:50.040
There was a study where they
were looking at muscle disuse,

02:36:50.040 --> 02:36:53.140
and it was something like
the local heat treatment

02:36:53.140 --> 02:36:57.720
prevented almost 40% of the
muscle atrophy from disuse.

02:36:57.720 --> 02:36:59.390
And it's funny 'cause
I used to use the sauna

02:36:59.390 --> 02:37:00.360
when I was injured and stuff.

02:37:00.360 --> 02:37:01.960
I would go in the sauna.

02:37:01.960 --> 02:37:04.290
Because I didn't know at the time

02:37:04.290 --> 02:37:05.450
'cause I was a graduate student,

02:37:05.450 --> 02:37:07.650
but I knew just from experiments

02:37:07.650 --> 02:37:10.470
that I'm not losing as much muscle.

02:37:10.470 --> 02:37:12.020
I feel better.

02:37:12.020 --> 02:37:14.331
At the time, I was reading
all about the growth hormone

02:37:14.331 --> 02:37:16.157
and stuff back then.

02:37:16.157 --> 02:37:18.300
And I knew about heat shock proteins,

02:37:18.300 --> 02:37:21.590
so I kind of knew, but that
data wasn't around yet,

02:37:21.590 --> 02:37:23.670
and so now we have the data.

02:37:23.670 --> 02:37:27.740
And I've always felt like
I wasn't losing my muscle,

02:37:27.740 --> 02:37:29.663
like I should've been,
when I was doing the sauna.

02:37:29.663 --> 02:37:32.600
And I was doing it
literally seven days a week.

02:37:32.600 --> 02:37:33.610
It was hardcore.

02:37:33.610 --> 02:37:35.530
- This is also during graduate school?

02:37:35.530 --> 02:37:38.183
- Yeah, now, I'm doing the sauna,

02:37:39.090 --> 02:37:42.890
like a bare minimum, I do
three, but I try to do four.

02:37:42.890 --> 02:37:45.160
It all depends on my schedule.

02:37:45.160 --> 02:37:46.713
I also like to do long runs.

02:37:47.680 --> 02:37:49.410
Long being like three miles,

02:37:49.410 --> 02:37:52.300
not like campaigns, which are long.

02:37:52.300 --> 02:37:54.470
But I really, for me,

02:37:54.470 --> 02:37:56.690
and we were talking about
this earlier off camera

02:37:56.690 --> 02:38:00.440
that the runs, for me, are for my brain.

02:38:00.440 --> 02:38:03.840
And I'd get this mind-wandering effect,

02:38:03.840 --> 02:38:05.370
where I daydream and I think about things.

02:38:05.370 --> 02:38:06.490
I work through problems.

02:38:06.490 --> 02:38:08.390
